window.HELP_TOC = {"groups":[{"title":"Get started","pages":[{"file":"index.html","title":"Welcome"},{"file":"workspace.html","title":"The Atelier window"},{"file":"shortcuts.html","title":"Keyboard shortcuts"}]},{"title":"Library","pages":[{"file":"import.html","title":"Import photos"},{"file":"library.html","title":"Organize and find"},{"file":"cull.html","title":"Cull: pick the keepers"},{"file":"photos.html","title":"Virtual copies, stacks and removing"},{"file":"notes.html","title":"Notes on photos"},{"file":"storage.html","title":"Storage locations"}]},{"title":"Develop","pages":[{"file":"develop.html","title":"Develop basics"},{"file":"basic.html","title":"Basic"},{"file":"tone-curve.html","title":"Tone Curve"},{"file":"hsl.html","title":"HSL / Color and B&W"},{"file":"color-grading.html","title":"Color Grading"},{"file":"detail.html","title":"Detail"},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","title":"Lens Corrections"},{"file":"transform.html","title":"Transform"},{"file":"effects.html","title":"Effects"},{"file":"calibration.html","title":"Calibration"}]},{"title":"Tools","pages":[{"file":"crop.html","title":"Crop & Straighten"},{"file":"remove.html","title":"Remove: Content-Aware, Heal and Clone"},{"file":"masking.html","title":"Masking"},{"file":"presets.html","title":"Presets"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","title":"Built-in preset lookbook"},{"file":"match-look.html","title":"Match Look"},{"file":"ai.html","title":"AI tools"},{"file":"lens-blur.html","title":"Lens Blur"},{"file":"hdr.html","title":"Single-image HDR"}]},{"title":"Share","pages":[{"file":"export.html","title":"Export"},{"file":"edit-in.html","title":"Edit In other apps"}]},{"title":"Set up","pages":[{"file":"settings.html","title":"Settings"},{"file":"chat-apps.html","title":"Connect a chat app"}]}],"sections":[{"file":"index.html","id":"","title":"Welcome","page":"Get started","text":"Atelier catalogs your photos and edits them without ever changing the originals. Search with the field on the left, or ⌘F."},{"file":"index.html","id":"workflow","title":"From card to website in four steps","page":"Welcome","text":"Import a shoot: ⇧⌘I, or drag a folder onto the window. Cull to pick the keepers from each burst, then rate and flag with single keys (1–5, P, X). Develop (D): start with Basic, then crop, mask and grade. Export (⇧⌘E) with the Website preset."},{"file":"index.html","id":"develop-tools","title":"Develop panels","page":"Welcome","text":"BasicWhite balance, exposure, contrast, presence. Tone CurveShape brightness point by point. HSL / Color and B&WChange one color; mix black and white. Color GradingTint shadows, midtones and highlights. DetailSharpening and noise reduction. Lens CorrectionsDistortion, fringes, dark corners. TransformStraighten leaning verticals. EffectsVignette, glow and film grain. CalibrationReshape the red, green and blue primaries."},{"file":"index.html","id":"tools","title":"Tools","page":"Welcome","text":"Crop & StraightenTrim, level, rotate, flip. RemoveContent-Aware, Heal and Clone: dust, specks, wires. MaskingGradients, brush, subject, people: edit part of a photo. PresetsLooks in one click, and your own. Match LookBorrow the look of another picture. AI toolsRemove objects, replace backgrounds, upscale. Lens BlurBlur the background and foreground from depth, saved as a new photo. Single-image HDRShape the tonal range of one original."},{"file":"index.html","id":"share","title":"Share and connect","page":"Welcome","text":"ExportJPEG for the web, TIFF for print. Edit In other appsPhotoshop, Affinity and back, stacked. Connect a chat appAsk OpenCode, Ollama or Claude about your library. SettingsAppearance, keys, import, AI and more."},{"file":"index.html","id":"library-tools","title":"Library","page":"Welcome","text":"The Atelier windowA tour of every part. Import photosFrom a card, drive or the NAS. CullKeep the best of each burst. Organize and findRate, flag, filter, collect. Virtual copies and stacksVersions without copying files; removing photos. Keyboard shortcutsEvery key, and how to change them. About the pictures. Before/after pictures are made from Rosh's own photos, a different one for each tool (portraits for masks, a sunrise for HDR, a noisy dusk shot for denoise), to show what each control does; they approximate Atelier's render. AI Denoise shows Atelier's own model on a real ISO 2800 RAW. The control drawings match the app's Graphite theme."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"","title":"The Atelier window","page":"Get started","text":"One window with three views: Grid to browse, Loupe to look closely, Develop to edit. HDR, an occasional tool, opens in the same window from the Photo menu. The parts around the photos stay in the same places in every view. ImportExport GridLoupeDevelopOrganize ⌄▯▮ LibraryAll Photographs1,284 ImportsSummer 201142Paris 2024310Kathakali18 LocationsMacintosh HDmegham offline CollectionsWebsitePrints 2026 Search filename, title, caption, keywordFilters ⌄ ⚑ ★★★★▤ 2 ★★★⧉ Copy 1 ★★RAW+JPG ⚑◫ 3 ★ ★★★✦ AI ✕ 42 photos · 1 selectedImported 42 photos Photo FileDSC_0004.NEFCameraNIKON D3100Lens11 mm ƒ/2.8 Rating and labelsRating★★★★FlagPicked Keywordslake, dawn, kayaks Summer 2011 · 42 photos · 1 selected · 1 of 42 · DSC_0004.NEF ⌄ Import and Export. See Import photos and Export. Grid, Loupe, Develop: switch views (G E D). Organize adds the selection to collections and imports. Show or hide the Inspector. Sidebar: all photos, your imports, drives and folders, collections. In Develop it becomes presets, snapshots and history; in HDR, the source and its saved result. Filter bar: search and filter by rating, flag and label. Grid, with badges under each photo. See Grid badges. Status bar: counts, and progress with Cancel for imports, exports and other background work. Inspector: the photo's details, rating, keywords. In Develop and HDR it becomes the editing panels. Drag its left edge to resize. Filmstrip: the current list, in every view. F6 hides it."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"views","title":"The three views","page":"The Atelier window","text":"Grid GBrowse, select, rate and organize. See Organize and find. Loupe EOne photo, large. Double-click to zoom to 100%; arrow keys move through photos. Develop DEdit the photo. See Develop basics. Photo › HDR… (⌃H, or right-click a photo › Edit in HDR) shows one photo's original in HDR, in this same window with the Filmstrip and panels. Done or Esc goes back to the view you opened it from; Grid, Loupe or Develop (G, E, D) also leave it. See Single-image HDR."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"right-click","title":"Right-click everything","page":"The Atelier window","text":"Photos (in the grid, loupe, Develop, HDR and the Filmstrip), sidebar entries, panel headers, masks, presets and color wheels all have right-click menus with the commands that apply to them. Every command is also in a menu in the menu bar."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"themes","title":"Themes","page":"The Atelier window","text":"Atelier › Settings… › Appearance picks the look: System, Light, Dark, Graphite (like Lightroom), Darkroom (near black) or Neutral Gray (for judging color)."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"session","title":"Atelier remembers where you were","page":"The Atelier window","text":"When you open Atelier, it comes back to the view, sidebar item, filters, selection and zoom you left, with the same photo open in Develop. If that photo is gone or its drive is off, you'll see the grid with a note in the status bar."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"catalog-recovery","title":"If the catalog cannot open","page":"The Atelier window","text":"⚠ Couldn’t Open Catalog ~/Pictures/Atelier/Atelier Catalog.atelier The reason appears here. Show in FinderQuitTry Again ↵ If Atelier cannot open its catalog, this screen replaces the splash. It names the location and reason. Try Again retries the same location after you resolve the problem, such as unavailable storage or access permissions. Show in Finder reveals the catalog, or its nearest available parent folder. Quit closes Atelier without making a catalog backup for the failed launch. Atelier does not delete, replace or reset the catalog, or automatically restore a backup. A catalog folder missing its database but containing previews, backups or other files is reported as a problem instead of opening an empty library. An empty folder, or one containing only Finder’s .DS_Store file, can finish a first launch that was interrupted. Use Show in Finder to locate the catalog’s Backups folder, where saved database copies are kept. Preserve a copy of the entire catalog package, including any database companion files, before attempting recovery. There is no built-in restore command yet. Library commands and settings become available once the catalog opens. Help › Atelier Help remains available while you resolve the problem. Next: Import photos · Keyboard shortcuts"},{"file":"shortcuts.html","id":"","title":"Keyboard shortcuts","page":"Get started","text":"Single keys work as in Lightroom. They act in the library window, never while you're typing. Change them (with ⌃, ⌥ or ⇧ if you like) in Settings › Keyboard; Help › Keyboard Shortcuts (⌘/) shows your current keys."},{"file":"shortcuts.html","id":"single-keys","title":"Single keys","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","text":"Views GGrid ELoupe DDevelop ZZoom: Fit and 100% F6Show or hide the Filmstrip Rating, flags and labels 1–5 · 0Rate 1 to 5 stars · clear P · X · UPicked · Rejected · no flag 6 7 8 9Red, Yellow, Green, Blue label Develop \\Show before (toggle) YBefore and after: off, side by side, slider RCrop & Straighten X (while cropping)Switch landscape and portrait VBlack & White (toggle) WWhite Balance Selector JShow clipping on the photo (toggle) Remove QOpen or close the Remove tool [ ] · ⇧[ ⇧]Brush size · feather /Next source (Content-Aware: another fill; Generative AI: ComfyUI makes another) AVisualize Spots Delete · EscDelete the selected spot · deselect, then close Masking ⇧WOpen or close Masking M · ⇧MLinear · Radial Gradient ⇧JColor Range mask KBrush OShow Overlay [ ] · ⇧[ ⇧]Brush size · feather ⌥ while paintingErase DeleteDelete the selected part Return · EscDone · cancel the tool or close Transform ⇧TGuided Upright: draw guides O (in Guided)Show Loupe DeleteDelete the selected guide EscClose Guided Upright Keyboard focus (Develop) Tab · ⇧TabNext · previous control in the focused area ⌃Tab · ⌃⇧TabNext · previous area (Presets, panels, Filmstrip) Arrows (· ⇧)Move the focused slider (bigger steps) ↑ ↓ · ReturnPresets: preview · apply Esc or click the photoArrows back to the photo Cull P · X · SpaceKeep · drop · switch ArrowsMove between photos and groups Return · EscFinish · cancel"},{"file":"shortcuts.html","id":"menu-shortcuts","title":"Menu shortcuts","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","text":"Library ⇧⌘IImport Photos… ⇧⌘EExport… ⌘S · ⌥⌘SWrite XMP for selection · all unsaved (in HDR, ⌘S is Save HDR) ⌘RRefresh Volumes DeleteRemove Photo… ⌘G ⌘E ⌘DGrid, Loupe, Develop HDR ⌃HPhoto › HDR…: open the photo in HDR ⌘S · ⇧⌘SSave HDR · Save HDR As New… EscDone: back to the view HDR was opened from (cancels a save in progress first) Photo ⌘'Create Virtual Copy ⇧⌘GShow Cull Group ⌥⌘EEdit in (first app) ⇧⌘MMatch Look to Reference… ⌥⇧⌘NAdd Note… Develop ⌘Z · ⇧⌘ZUndo · Redo ⇧⌘C · ⇧⌘VCopy · Paste Settings ⇧⌘RReset All Settings ⇧⌘UAuto White Balance ⌘[ · ⌘]Rotate left · right ⇧⌘NNew Preset… View and app ⌘= · ⌘-Zoom in · out ⌘0 · ⌥⌘1Zoom to Fit · 100% ⌥⌘NShow Notes ⌘,Settings ⌘?Atelier Help (this window) ⌘/Keyboard Shortcuts"},{"file":"shortcuts.html","id":"mouse","title":"Mouse and trackpad","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","text":"Double-click the photoZoom to 100% and back at that spot Pinch, or ⌥/⌘ + scrollZoom freely Drag, scroll, Space + dragMove around when zoomed Double-click a slider's nameReset it ⇧ double-click a Tone slider's nameAuto for that slider ⌥ drag a sliderSee what it affects (⌥-drag views)"},{"file":"import.html","id":"","title":"Import photos","page":"Library","text":"Bring a shoot into Atelier from a card, a drive or the NAS. Leave the files where they are, or copy them into your library folder."},{"file":"import.html","id":"open","title":"Start an import","page":"Import photos","text":"Any of these opens the Import dialog: File › Import Photos… (⇧⌘I) or the Import button in the toolbar. Drag folders or photos from Finder onto the Atelier window. Right-click an import in the sidebar › Import More Photos Into This…"},{"file":"import.html","id":"dialog","title":"The Import dialog","page":"Import photos","text":"Import Photos ●Choose Photos— ○ Cull—○ Import 📁 100NZ6 128 ⊗ + Add… IMPORT INTO New import100NZ6 FILE HANDLING Leave files where they areCopy into my library folder APPLY DURING IMPORT Develop settingsPortra 400Tone Curve presetMatteColor Grading presetNone CULLING Cull before importing Import Preset: Zf Field 128 photos · 5.9 GB · copied into Atelier Library · into “100NZ6” CancelImport 128 Photos Steps: where you are. Choose Photos › Import, or Choose Photos › Cull › Import with culling on. The dialog keeps the same size on every step. Sources: each folder or file you chose, with how many photos it has; ⊗ takes it out, Add… adds another. You can also drop folders or photos anywhere on the dialog. Photos: a thumbnail of every photo found (a RAW and its JPEG once). Import Into: a new import or an existing one. A new one is named after the first folder; the name is real text, so you can add to it or change it. File Handling: Leave files where they are (on this Mac or the NAS; nothing is moved) or Copy into my library folder, in dated folders, each copy checked against the original. Apply During Import: for every photo, a Develop preset, then a Tone Curve preset, then a Color Grading preset, and keywords. See Presets during import. Culling: Cull before importing groups similar shots so you pick the keepers first. See Cull. Import Preset (see Import Presets), what will happen, and the button, which says how many photos will be imported and stays dimmed until there's at least one"},{"file":"import.html","id":"apply","title":"Presets during import","page":"Import photos","text":"The three menus under Apply During Import are applied to every new photo in order: Develop settings, then Tone Curve preset, then Color Grading preset. The Tone Curve and Color Grading lists are the ones in those panels' Preset menus, so a panel preset changes only its panel and leaves the Develop preset's other settings alone. Where two overlap, the later one wins. Each one is its own step in the photo's History: Import Preset: Portra 400, Import Tone Curve Preset: Matte, Import Color Grading Preset: Teal & Orange. Click an earlier step to see the photo without the later ones."},{"file":"import.html","id":"import-presets","title":"Import Presets","page":"Import photos","text":"Save the dialog's settings to use again, as in Lightroom Classic. Click Import Preset at the bottom left: Save Current Settings as New Preset…: name it. A preset keeps File Handling (and the library folder for copies), Apply During Import and Culling, but not the photos chosen or the import's name. Using a name that's already taken asks before replacing it. Choose a preset by name to set all the panels at once. Change anything and the menu shows (edited). Update Preset saves the change. Rename Preset… and Delete Preset change the chosen preset. None leaves the settings as they are and stops following a preset. If a preset names a Develop, Tone Curve or Color Grading preset you've since deleted, that menu is set to None and the dialog says so."},{"file":"import.html","id":"progress","title":"While it imports","page":"Import photos","text":"✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ 137 of 412 photos copied DSC_0425.NEF COPIED6.1 of 18.4 GB SPEED180 MB/s TIME LEFTAbout 1 minute Each copy is checked against the original. Thumbnails are made once every photo is in.Run in Background Cancel Import The photos being imported, faint and grey until each one is in, then in full color with a check. The wall moves on by itself to keep the photos being copied in view. After culling it shows the photos you culled. How many are copied (or added, when files stay where they are), a progress bar and the file being copied. Copied, Speed and Time left. Run in Background and Cancel Import. The dialog stays open and shows how far it has got: how many photos are copied (or added) in large figures, a progress bar, the file being copied, and how much is copied, the speed and the time left. Cancel Import (Esc) stops it; photos already copied stay in the catalog. Run in Background closes the dialog and keeps the progress, with Cancel, in the status bar. When every file is in, the dialog closes, the new import is selected in the sidebar and its thumbnails are made. When it's done the status bar sums up what was imported, what was already in the catalog, and any files it couldn't read; Show Problems lists those with the reason. With Cull before importing on, Continue to Cull moves the same dialog to the Cull step. Back returns to your settings as you left them. Copied while you cull. As soon as the Cull step has listed the photos, Atelier starts copying them to your"},{"file":"import.html","id":"raw-jpeg","title":"RAW+JPEG pairs","page":"Import photos","text":"If your camera saves a RAW and a JPEG of each shot, Atelier shows them as one photo, the RAW, marked RAW+JPG. Ratings, flags, labels and keywords apply to both; editing and export use the RAW. Turn this off in Settings › Import › Show RAW+JPEG pairs as one photo."},{"file":"import.html","id":"defaults","title":"Defaults","page":"Import photos","text":"Atelier › Settings… › Import sets whether new imports leave or copy files, and where the library folder is. The dialog remembers your last choices. Next: Cull: pick the keepers · Organize and find"},{"file":"library.html","id":"","title":"Organize and find","page":"Library","text":"Browse in the grid, look closely in the loupe, rate and flag with single keys, and find any photo with the filter bar. SidebarGrid and LoupeRate, flag, labelThumbnail badgesFilter and searchInspectorCollections and importsPrivate importsFilmstripXMP sidecarsDrives and the NAS"},{"file":"library.html","id":"sidebar","title":"Sidebar","page":"Organize and find","text":"Library › All PhotographsEvery photo in the catalog. ImportsOne entry per import, named after its folder. Right-click to Rename…, Import More Photos Into This…, Cull… or Delete Import…. Deleting the list keeps the photos in the catalog and on disk, unless you tick Also delete the photos and move their files to the Trash. That box always starts unticked, and ticking it shows how many files will go to the Trash before you confirm with Delete Import and Files. LocationsYour drives, NAS shares and cloud drive folders (the ones you add in Settings › Storage, and any you import from), each with the folders inside it; a folder location such as Photos SSD/Portfolio has its own row with its own name. A green dot is connected, grey is offline (its photos stay browsable from their previews), orange is a share you added that hasn't been connected yet. Right-click one to Connect, Disconnect or Show in Finder. CollectionsYour own lists. A photo can be in any number of them. + makes a new one. Imports and collections marked with a lock are private."},{"file":"library.html","id":"grid-loupe","title":"Grid and Loupe","page":"Organize and find","text":"G · E · DGrid · Loupe (one photo, large) · Develop. Also the toolbar buttons and ⌘G ⌘E ⌘D; ⌃H opens HDR. Double-click a thumbnail, or ReturnOpen it in Loupe. Return or Esc goes back to the grid. ← →Previous or next photo. Click · ⌘-click · ⇧-clickSelect one · add or remove one · select a run. Double-click in Loupe, or ZZoom to 100% and back. Drag to move around. Right-click a photoThe photo menu: open, edit, rate, flag, label, develop settings, presets, collections, Edit In, AI, Export, Remove Photo…"},{"file":"library.html","id":"rate","title":"Rate, flag, label","page":"Organize and find","text":"Single keys, as in Lightroom. They act on the selected photos (change them in Settings › Keyboard). Rating 1 … 5★ to ★★★★★ 0Clear the rating Flags PPicked ⚑ XRejected ✕ (dimmed in the grid) URemove the flag Color labels 6 7 8 9● Red · ● Yellow · ● Green · ● Blue"},{"file":"library.html","id":"badges","title":"Thumbnail badges","page":"Organize and find","text":"Thumbnails in the grid and the Filmstrip carry small badges in their corners, as in Lightroom's grid: Top left: 2 ⚑ ✕The photo's number in the Filmstrip, and its flag: picked, or rejected (the photo is also dimmed). Top right: ▤ 3An original with versions stacked on it (edits, AI results, virtual copies): 3 photos in all. See Stacks. ◪ Edit ✦ AI ⧉ Copy 1An Edit In version, an AI result, a virtual copy. RAW+JPGA RAW shown with the JPEG shot alongside it, as one photo. ◫ 4Culled with 3 other shots. ⇧⌘G shows the group (see Cull). Bottom left: ★★★★ ●The rating and the color label. Bottom rightEdited in Develop (sliders icon), cropped or straightened (crop icon), has masks (dashed circle), a cloud with an arrow when the original is still only in a cloud drive folder (it downloads when you open it in Develop), and ⚠ the original's drive is offline. Small thumbnails show names as icons and stars as “★ 4”. At the Filmstrip's smallest height only the flag, the label, the rating and one mark (offline first) remain. Hover over a thumbnail to read its file name and what its badges mean. The photo you're working on has a solid accent ring with a thin white line inside; the other selected photos have a lighter ring. This is the same in the grid, the Filmstrip and Cull, in every theme."},{"file":"library.html","id":"filter","title":"Filter and search","page":"Organize and find","text":"The bar above the photos narrows the current list: Search: type part of a filename, title, caption or keyword. Filters: a minimum Rating (★ + to ★★★★★), a Flag (Picked, Unflagged, Rejected), a Label and Edits (Edited or Unedited in Develop). The button shows how many are on. In the grid, the − + slider at the right of the bar makes thumbnails smaller or larger. Filters apply to whatever is selected in the sidebar, and to the Filmstrip. The Filmstrip's Filters button sets the same rules. In a narrow column, the filter pickers move into the Filters menu. When only the search field fits, the Filmstrip menu still gives you the same filters."},{"file":"library.html","id":"inspector","title":"Inspector","page":"Organize and find","text":"The right panel in Grid and Loupe (toggle it with the Inspector toolbar button). Its sections open and close like Develop's panels: click a header, or right-click one for Solo Mode, Expand All and Collapse All. Long names end in “…”; hover to read them in full. From the top: Histogram of the photo as edited, with clipping triangles, and under it the ISO, focal length, aperture and shutter speed it was taken with. Hover over that line for the camera, lens and exposure compensation. Editing it is in Develop. File: name, Paired With (the JPEG shot with a RAW, shown as one photo), copy name, folder, drive, dimensions and file size. Camera: capture time, camera, lens, exposure, ISO, focal length, exposure bias, flash, exposure program and metering mode. Rating, flag and label, Keywords (comma separated; Apply Keywords adds them to every selected photo), and Title and caption. In Loupe the photo itself also shows its name, when it was taken, its size and its camera settings in the corner, as Lightroom's Loupe info does."},{"file":"library.html","id":"collections","title":"Collections and imports","page":"Organize and find","text":"The Organize toolbar menu works on the selected photos: New Collection with Selection…, Add to a collection, Add to Import, and, when you're in a collection or import, Remove from This Collection or Remove from This Import. Removing from a list never removes the photo from the catalog. For photos on a network share, right-click the import or collection in the sidebar for Keep on This Mac… and Free Up Space (see Keep on This Mac)."},{"file":"library.html","id":"private","title":"Private imports and collections","page":"Organize and find","text":"Some shoots you want to open on purpose, not see every time you browse. Make their import (or a collection) private, and its photos stay out of sight until you ask for them. Make it private: right-click the import or collection in the sidebar › Make Private…, then Make Private to confirm. Its photos now appear nowhere in Atelier: not in the sidebar, All Photographs, other imports or collections, folders that hold only private photos, the Filmstrip's source menu, search, or any count. See them: View › Show Private Photos… (with all your other photos) or View › Show Only Private Photos… (nothing else in the sidebar or grid), and confirm with Touch ID or your Mac's login password. Private imports show a lock after their name, and Hide Private appears at the top of the sidebar. Switching between the two doesn't ask again. These View menu items are there only when something is private. Hide them again: View › Hide Private Photos or Hide Private in the sidebar. They also hide on their own after 10 minutes without using Atelier (change it in Settings › General › Private Photos), when your Mac sleeps or locks, and every time Atelier opens. If a private photo is open, Atelier goes back to the grid. Stop it being private: while private photos are shown, right-click the import › Make Not Private. A photo in a private list stays hidden even when it's also in an ordinary import or collection, and so do its virtual copies, edits and the JPEG of its RAW+JPEG pair. Not encryption. This keeps"},{"file":"library.html","id":"filmstrip","title":"Filmstrip","page":"Organize and find","text":"The strip of thumbnails along the bottom, in every view, showing the current list in the same order as the grid. Its header, from the left: Source: the list's name and how many photos it shows. Click it to switch to All Photographs, one of the recent imports, a collection, or a folder (under its drive) without leaving the view you're in. How many photos are selected, which one you're on (“2 of 12”) and its file name. Filters: the same Flag, Rating, Color Label and Edits rules as the bar above the photos. When any are on, the button turns blue and lists them (“Picked · ★3+”); the ⓧ next to it clears them. Sort: Capture Time, Import Order, File Name, Rating or Edit Time. The arrow button next to it switches between ascending and descending. Versions stacked on a photo always stay right after it. The grid uses the same order, and Atelier remembers it. Click, ⌘-click and ⇧-click select, as in the grid. Right-click for the photo menu. Drag its top edge to make thumbnails bigger or smaller. Clicking a photo that is already in view never scrolls the strip; it scrolls only to bring the next photo in from the edge. Hide or show it with the small tab in the middle of the bottom edge, View › Show Filmstrip, or F6."},{"file":"library.html","id":"xmp","title":"XMP sidecars","page":"Organize and find","text":"Ratings, flags, labels, keywords, titles and captions live in the catalog. To let Lightroom, Bridge or Capture One see them, write them to .xmp files next to the originals: Library › Write XMP for Selection (⌘S) or Write All Unsaved XMP (⌥⌘S). Or turn on Settings › Metadata › Automatically write changes into XMP sidecars."},{"file":"library.html","id":"recover-edits","title":"Recover edits, history and snapshots","page":"Organize and find","text":"Writing XMP also saves the current Atelier Develop settings and masks. Develop edits mark the sidecar unsaved; automatic writing includes them. To back up edits made before this feature, select those photos and choose Library › Write XMP for Selection. If you lose the catalog, import the originals with their sidecars into a new catalog. Atelier restores the matching current edits, saved history and snapshots before making previews, even when an import preset is selected. Photos already in the catalog keep their existing edits. RAW and JPEG files with the same name keep separate Develop entries in their shared sidecar; ratings and other standard metadata remain shared. Keep filenames unchanged for recovery. Old metadata-only sidecars still work. Unreadable foreign metadata alone does not block import. Unsupported or damaged Atelier recovery data appears in the import problems; Atelier leaves the sidecar untouched instead of silently replacing your edits. A write that cannot safely preserve existing recovery data also reports a problem. If another, older Atelier version changes current edits while leaving saved history behind, recovery reports the mismatch instead of combining incompatible states. Keep history backups current using this version of Atelier. New sidecar writes include retained History steps, the selected step and redo steps, plus snapshot names, dates and saved looks. Earlier sidecars containing only current edits recover one state, shown as Recovered from XMP. W"},{"file":"library.html","id":"volumes","title":"Drives and the NAS","page":"Organize and find","text":"Atelier remembers photos by drive, not by where it's mounted, so plugging a drive in somewhere else doesn't break anything. When the NAS or a drive is off, its photos stay in the grid from their previews, marked Offline. Library › Refresh Volumes (⌘R) checks again after you connect one. To add a NAS or a drive, see Storage locations. See also: Virtual copies, stacks and removing · Keyboard shortcuts"},{"file":"cull.html","id":"","title":"Cull: pick the keepers","page":"Library","text":"Atelier groups bursts and repeated shots of the same scene, suggests the best of each group, and lets you keep or drop shots with single keys."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"start","title":"Start culling","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"Before importingTick Cull before importing in the Import dialog; the button becomes Continue to Culling… and the same dialog moves on to the Cull step. Only keepers go into the import entry. The rest are still cataloged, flagged Rejected, so you can look again later. After importingRight-click an import in the sidebar › Cull…, or Library › Cull Import…. Keepers are flagged Picked and the rest Rejected; nothing is removed."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"view","title":"The Cull view","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"MatchSame FrameSimilar Shots GroupingTight Loose Pick ByBalanced ▾☐ Advanced checks (slower) Similar shots · 4 photos ✓ Same frame · 2 photos ✓ Single shots SuggestedKeep Sharpness 100% of the group's best · Exposure OK Click to look · Space or double-click keeps or drops · P keep · X drop · arrows move☑ Write reasons to notes Cancel Import 5 Photos Match and Grouping at the top. Same Frame groups only shots framed alike (someone blinked, moved slightly). Similar Shots (the default) also groups the same scene reframed or zoomed a little. The Grouping slider, from Tight to Loose, regroups instantly. It starts at Tight, so only shots that really match are grouped; slide toward Loose to group more. Groups as rows. Keepers have a check; dropped shots are dimmed. Single shots are listed last and kept. Drag the line between the groups and the large photo to make the list wider or narrower; the photos wrap to fit. The focused photo, large, with what was measured: its sharpness against the group's best (on the faces, or the main subject), how many people have their eyes open, and its exposure. Atelier marks the best one by Pick By Suggested and starts it as the keeper. A dropped photo also says why, in orange: the same reasons its note will get. Keys, Write reasons to notes, and Cancel and Import N Photos (or Done when culling an import). Before importing, this line shows the photos being copied to your library while you cull. Pick By and Advanced checks (slower): how the keeper of e"},{"file":"cull.html","id":"zoom","title":"Zoom in on the photo","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"The large photo zooms as in Loupe and Develop: double-click it or press Z to switch between Fit and 100%, pinch or hold ⌥ and scroll to zoom freely, and drag to move around. The bar under the photo has the same Fit, Fill, 100% and 200% buttons. The zoom stays as you move to the next photo, so you can check the same eyes or the same spot of focus across a group. Zooming here doesn't change Loupe's or Develop's zoom."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"subject","title":"Show Subject: what was measured","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"Tick Show Subject in the bar under the photo to see what Cull measured. A dashed orange box shows the subject its sharpness was read on: the faces; the people when no face is found (sunglasses, a face turned away or too small); Vision's main object when there are no people; or the whole frame when nothing stood out. When a group's shots aren't all measured on faces, every shot is measured on the same area, so near-identical shots compare the same thing. Each face gets a box in green (eyes open), yellow (half closed), red (closed) or gray (too small or turned to check), dashed when MediaPipe found it, and a small number. Nothing is written on the photo: an orange line under it says what the subject is (for example \"Subject (orange): 4 faces\"), the next line says what each face's number means (\"① Eyes open, smiling ② Half closed\"), and the caption above gives the sharpness against the group's sharpest shot. If a photo says \"Subject softer\" but looks as sharp as the keeper, compare the boxes on both shots."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"background-faces","title":"Blurred faces in the background","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"A face much softer than the sharpest face in the same photo is taken as background: someone behind your subject, out of focus because you focused on the subject. Cull leaves it out of the subject, the eye and smile checks and the reasons, so a passer-by blinking behind you never drops your shot. With Show Subject on, its box is faint and dotted, the line under the photo says \"1 blurred background face left out\", and its number reads \"Background, out of focus: left out\". The line is drawn from measurements on test photos: faces in focus measure at least about 40% of the sharpest face's detail (smooth skin reads lower than a beard), blurred background faces under 25%. A face counts as background under 20%, or under 35% when it is also less than half as wide as the sharpest face (farther away). Within a group, a person counts as background only when they read that way in most of the shots, so one shot where focus slipped to the person behind stays a soft shot of your subject, not a sharp shot of them."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"markers","title":"Note markers","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"A note can point at a spot on the photo. In the notes panel (⌥⌘N) click Pin to Photo under a note, then click the photo where it belongs; Esc stops. The spot shows on the photo as a numbered marker, and the same number shows beside the note; a line under the photo lists each marker's number with the note's first words. Remove Pin takes it off. Cull pins its own reasons to the face with closed eyes. Turn Note Markers (in the bar under the photo) off to hide them all. Markers show only here, in the Cull view and when you look at a group again: never in Loupe, Develop or anything you export."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"pick","title":"Picking the keeper","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"Pick By changes which photo of each group is suggested; the groups stay the same. Changing it suggests again at once, nothing is read again. It's remembered for the next cull. BalancedThe default. Fewest people with eyes closed, then a sharp subject, good faces and exposure; Apple's aesthetics score breaks a tie. A closed eye counts for more than a small loss of sharpness: a soft photo can be sharpened a little, a closed eye can't be opened. Sharpest SubjectThe sharpest faces (with Advanced checks, the eyes themselves), or the main subject when there are no faces. Background detail doesn't count, so a busy sharp background can't beat a sharp face on a soft one. Everyone's Eyes OpenThe fewest people with eyes closed, then half closed, then the sharpest. Best ExposureThe least clipped highlights and crushed shadows, closest to the group's usual brightness, then the sharpest. Best ExpressionEyes open first, then the most smiles, then the sharpest. Smiles are read by the Advanced checks, so choosing it turns them on; until they finish it picks like Everyone's Eyes Open. ClassicAtelier's earlier pick: the sharpest whole frame plus the best face's quality."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"find-faces","title":"Find Faces","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"Find Faces, beside Pick By, chooses which face detector finds the faces Cull checks: Apple Vision (built into macOS), MediaPipe (Google's detector, built into Atelier; it looks inside each person found, so it can catch a face behind sunglasses or a small one), or Both, the default: Vision first, then MediaPipe inside any person Vision found no face for. Every photo is read with each, so switching only suggests again. With Show Subject on, a face MediaPipe found says \"found by MediaPipe\"."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"eyes","title":"How eyes are checked","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"For every face, Atelier measures how open the eyes are and compares each person with the same person in the group's other shots. So naturally narrow eyes, glasses and squinting smiles aren't called closed: only eyes much less open than that person's own widest are. Faces turned away, and faces too small to see at the standard size, are left out and the caption says \"not checked\"."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"advanced","title":"Advanced checks (slower)","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"Off by default. With it on, Atelier reads every photo in a group again from a large copy, three to four times the standard size: small faces in group shots get their eyes checked, and sharpness compares the eyes themselves instead of whole faces. A face model built into Atelier (Google's MediaPipe Face Landmarker, which runs on your Mac) also reads each face's blinks and smile. Its blink score then decides whether eyes are open, half closed or closed, again against the same person in the group's other shots, and is more reliable than the eye outline on small or squinting faces. The caption adds \"Smiling: 3 of 5\", and a dropped photo can say \"Fewer smiles\". It takes roughly a fifth of a second more per grouped photo, starting with the focused group; single shots are skipped, since they're always kept. The top of the view shows its progress, and switching it off stops it. Your Keep and Drop clicks stay as they are when it finishes."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"reasons","title":"Reasons on dropped photos","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"With Write reasons to notes on (the default), finishing the cull gives each photo not kept a note by Cull with a bullet for each measured reason, compared with its group's keeper, for example: Not kept: group of 6 (Similar shots), keeper DSC_0102.NEF. - Eyes closed: 2 of 5 people (keeper: none) - Subject softer: 58% of the keeper's sharpness, looks like motion blur - 1.3 stops darker than the keeper Only what was measured is written. Kept photos get no note. Culling again replaces Cull's note, unless you edited it: then yours stays and the new one is added. A photo kept later on another look keeps its note, marked Kept later."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"keys","title":"Keys","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"← →Previous or next photo. ↑ ↓The group above or below, at the same place in the row. PKeep. XDrop. Space or double-click a thumbnailSwitch between keep and drop. Z or double-click the photoZoom between Fit and 100%. ReturnFinish. EscCancel (while a note waits to be pinned, stops that instead). The keys work as soon as the Cull view opens; there's nothing to click first. The list only scrolls when the photo you move to isn't fully in view, and then just enough to show it. Clicking a photo never scrolls. A group can keep several shots. A RAW+JPEG pair counts as one shot: keeping or dropping it applies to both."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"review","title":"Look at a group again","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"Every photo culled with others shows ◫ 4 (the group's size) in the grid. Select it and choose Photo › Show Cull Group (⇧⌘G), or right-click › Show Cull Group, to see the keeper next to every shot it beat, and change your mind. See also: Import photos · Ratings and flags"},{"file":"photos.html","id":"","title":"Virtual copies, stacks and removing","page":"Library","text":"Make several versions of one photo without duplicating the file, see how versions stack, and take photos out of the catalog or move them to the Trash."},{"file":"photos.html","id":"virtual-copies","title":"Virtual copies","page":"Virtual copies, stacks and removing","text":"A virtual copy is a second set of edits on the same file: a color and a black and white version, or two different crops. DSC_0004 ⧉ Copy 1 Make one: right-click › Create Virtual Copy, or Photo › Create Virtual Copy (⌘'). Each selected photo gets one. A copy starts with the photo's current edits, rating, label, keywords and collections (not its flag), then has its own of everything. Editing a copy never changes the original. Copies sit right after the original in the grid with a ⧉ Copy 1 badge. Remove one: right-click › Remove Virtual Copy…. Only the copy's settings go; the file is untouched."},{"file":"photos.html","id":"stacks","title":"Stacks","page":"Virtual copies, stacks and removing","text":"Edit In versions, AI results and virtual copies stack on their original: they sort right after it, join the same imports and collections, and the original shows the count (▤ 3)."},{"file":"photos.html","id":"remove","title":"Remove or delete photos","page":"Virtual copies, stacks and removing","text":"Select photos and press Delete, or right-click › Remove Photo… (Remove 3 Photos… with several). The dialog says what will happen, with numbers: how many files, how many virtual copies go with them, how many are on a drive that isn't connected. Choose: RemoveThe default. Takes the photos out of the catalog, with their edits and metadata. The files stay where they are. Delete from DiskAlso moves the files to the Trash. CancelNothing changes (or press Esc). With RAW+JPEG pairs, the dialog has a checkbox per kind of file (for example “RAW (.NEF) 12 files” and “JPEG (.JPG) 12 files”); untick one to keep those files. Nothing is erased silently. Files only go to the Trash. A network share like the NAS has no Trash, so Atelier asks before deleting from it immediately. You'll hear the Dock's poof when something is removed from Atelier, and Finder's Trash sound when files go to the Trash. Turn this off in Settings › General › Feedback. See also: Organize and find · Edit In"},{"file":"notes.html","id":"","title":"Notes on photos","page":"Library","text":"Write yourself a sticky note on any photo: what to fix, why you kept it, a list of things to try. Notes float over the photo in Library, Loupe, Develop and Cull, and never change the photo or its zoom. NotesDSC_0004.NEF+× Second pass: keep for the shop. ☐ Crop tighter ☑ Fix the horizon You · 12 Nov 2026, 18:40 Rejected: eyes closed in 2 of 3 faces Cull · 12 Nov 2026, 17:40 The notes panel in a corner of the photo: newest note first, each signed with who wrote it and when."},{"file":"notes.html","id":"show","title":"Show and hide the notes","page":"Notes on photos","text":"View › Show Notes (⌥⌘N) shows or hides the panel. It stays shown or hidden in every view and after a relaunch. The panel shows the notes of the photo in front: the focused photo in the Grid, the photo in Loupe and Develop, and the focused shot in Cull. Photos with notes carry a small yellow note mark on their thumbnail in the Grid and Filmstrip. Culling writes its own note, by Cull, on each photo it didn't keep, with a bullet for each reason (eyes closed, softer than the keeper…). See Reasons on dropped photos. Move it: drag the panel's top strip; it snaps to the nearest corner. Resize it: drag the grip on the corner facing the middle of the photo. Atelier remembers both."},{"file":"notes.html","id":"write","title":"Write a note","page":"Notes on photos","text":"Click + in the panel, or choose Photo › Add Note… (⌥⇧⌘N), or right-click a photo and choose Add Note… The panel opens if it was hidden. Type. Each note is saved as you type. If a change can't be saved (for example, the catalog's disk is full), a red line under the panel's header says so until a later change saves. Press Esc or ⌘Return, or click Done, to finish. A note left empty goes away. Several photos at once: select them in the Grid first. The panel says “Adding to 12 photos”, and the note goes to each of them when you finish. Change a note: click it. Delete one: point at it and click ⋯ › Delete, or right-click it. It goes at once, with Undo in the panel for ten seconds."},{"file":"notes.html","id":"formatting","title":"Lists, checkboxes and bold","page":"Notes on photos","text":"Notes are plain text with a few marks, as in Notes and many chat apps: - or * A bullet. Return starts the next one; Return on an empty bullet ends the list. 1. A numbered list; Return carries on with the next number. [] A checkbox. Click a box in the note to tick it, without opening the note. Tab, ⇧TabMoves a list line in or out (two levels). ⌘B, ⌘IBold (**word**) and italic (*word*). ⇧ReturnA new line without a bullet. While you type, the marks stay visible but dimmed, so what you see is what is saved. Once you finish, the note shows real bullets, numbers and checkboxes. Prefer buttons? While a note is open, the strip above the text has Bullets, Numbered list, Checkboxes, Bold and Italic. A list button turns the selected lines into that list, or back into plain lines. An empty note also shows a hint with the typed shortcuts."},{"file":"notes.html","id":"find","title":"Find photos by their notes","page":"Notes on photos","text":"The Library search field also searches notes. The filter bar's Notes menu (and the Filmstrip's Filter pop-up) shows only photos with notes, without notes, or with Unticked Checkboxes: your to-do list across the catalog."},{"file":"notes.html","id":"copies","title":"Virtual copies, XMP and privacy","page":"Notes on photos","text":"Each virtual copy has its own notes. A new copy, Edit In version or AI result starts with the original's notes. Notes are saved in the photo's XMP sidecar in Atelier's own field, so they come back if you import the photo into another Atelier catalog. They never go into the Caption or any field other apps show, and Export never includes them. Undo (⌘Z) takes back a note a chat app added. In the Cull view a note can be pinned to a spot on the photo, shown there as a numbered marker. See Note markers."},{"file":"notes.html","id":"chat-apps","title":"Notes from chat apps","page":"Notes on photos","text":"A connected chat app can read a photo's notes and add one (it is signed with the app's name, such as “Claude”). It cannot change or delete your notes. See Connect a chat app."},{"file":"notes.html","id":"settings","title":"Font, color and opacity","page":"Notes on photos","text":"In Atelier › Settings… › Appearance › Notes: Notes fontThe handwriting-style Noteworthy (comes with macOS) or the system font. ColorSticky-note yellow (the default), pink, green, blue, orange or purple, or any color from the color well. The text turns light on a dark color. Opacity85% to start, so the photo shows faintly through the paper; 100% is solid. The text always stays solid. The note is a flat see-through fill, so it never slows the photo down. Beyond Lightroom. Lightroom Classic has no per-photo notes; people use the Caption field, which is exported with the photo. Atelier's notes are private and stay out of exports. See also: Organize and find · Cull: pick the keepers · Virtual copies"},{"file":"storage.html","id":"","title":"Storage locations","page":"Library","text":"Atelier has no cloud of its own. You add the places you already keep photos (a NAS, an external drive, a folder, a cloud drive folder such as Dropbox) and a bucket for catalog backups if you like, in Atelier › Settings… › Storage, and Atelier uses them. Nothing is set up until you add it. Locations ▤ meghamNetwork share · megham.local/Photos Connected12,408 photos ▭ PortfolioDrive · Photos SSD/Portfolio Offline1,920 photos ☁ Dropbox ShootsCloud drive folder · Dropbox/Shoots Connected846 photos +− Settings › Storage: each location with where it is, whether it is connected, how many photos it holds and how full it is. Click one to see its details below the list. Add a locationA NASCloud drive foldersDetailsCache of originalsKeep on This MacWhen the original changedSmart previewsOfflineBack up the catalogObject storage (S3)What stays on this MacRemove"},{"file":"storage.html","id":"add","title":"Add a location","page":"Storage locations","text":"Open Atelier › Settings… › Storage and click +. Choose Drive or Folder on This Mac, Network Share (NAS), Cloud Drive Folder or Object Storage (S3) (for backups, see below), then Continue. Pick the place: a drive or folder in the Open panel, a server found on your network or an address such as smb://megham.local/Photos, or a cloud drive found on this Mac. Check the name (it starts as the share's or folder's name) and click Add Location. You can add only a folder on a drive, for example Photos SSD/Portfolio. Photos Atelier already has from that drive stay exactly where they are; a location never moves or renames anything."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"nas","title":"A NAS","page":"Storage locations","text":"Servers that share over SMB on your network are listed by name. Double-click one, or select it and click Connect. macOS asks for the user name and password the first time, in its own window, and keeps them in your Keychain. If you saved the password in Finder before (Remember this password in my keychain), nothing is asked. Atelier never stores a password itself. Connect when Atelier opens (on by default for a share) connects it at launch, quietly, with the Keychain's password. If the NAS is off, Atelier opens as usual and the status bar says it is offline."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"cloud","title":"Cloud drive folders","page":"Storage locations","text":"iCloud Drive, Dropbox, Google Drive, OneDrive, Box, Synology Drive and other cloud drives work through their own Mac app, which keeps a folder in sync. Atelier never signs in to them; it reads the files in that folder. The cloud drives on this Mac are listed by name. Select one and click Use All of Dropbox (or double-click it), or Choose Folder… to pick only the folder where you keep photos. Photos are remembered by cloud drive, not by where the Mac keeps its folder, so signing in again or moving to a new Mac keeps them linked. A photo that is still only in the cloud shows a cloud mark on its thumbnail. Its preview shows as usual; when you open it in Develop, Atelier asks the cloud drive's app to download it, shows the download in the status bar with Cancel, and you can edit as soon as it arrives. Nothing is downloaded just by browsing. The Import dialog says how much it will download before you import from a cloud folder, for example “412 photos · 18.4 GB · 11.6 GB will be downloaded”. If the cloud drive's app signs out or is removed, the location shows Signed Out and its photos stay browsable from their previews."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"details","title":"Details","page":"Storage locations","text":"NameWhat the sidebar's Locations and the Storage pane call it. Press Return to keep a new name. WhereThe folder on this Mac while it is connected. Connect · DisconnectMount or unmount a share. Also in the sidebar: right-click the location under Locations, or click the small Connect button on an offline share's row. Show in FinderOpens the location's folder."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"cache","title":"Cache of originals","page":"Storage locations","text":"When you open a photo from a network share in Develop, Atelier reads its original once, start to end, into a folder on this Mac, and edits from that copy, so sliders, zooming in and Export never wait on the network. The first time, the status bar shows Reading DSC_1.NEF · 48 MB from megham… with a progress bar and Cancel; the photo's preview stays on screen meanwhile. The photos just before and after it are read in the background, so stepping through a shoot on the NAS feels local after the first one. Before using a copy, Atelier checks the original's size and date on the share (no download). If the file changed there, it is read again; if its contents differ from what was imported, Atelier stops and asks (see When the original changed). When the share is off, a photo with a copy on this Mac still opens in Develop at full quality, and the status bar says Using the copy on this Mac from Nov 17; megham is offline, so changes there can't be checked. Set its size in Atelier › Settings… › Storage › Cache of Originals (20 GB to start; it never takes more than a quarter of the free space). The copies opened longest ago go first. Clear Cache… removes them; the originals are never touched. Photos you keep on this Mac stay unless you also tick Also remove kept photos in that alert (it starts unticked). Photos in a cloud drive folder aren't copied: the cloud drive's own download is their copy on this Mac."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"keep","title":"Keep on This Mac","page":"Storage locations","text":"Going somewhere without the NAS? Right-click a folder, an import, a collection or a network share in the sidebar, then choose Keep on This Mac…; Atelier first says how much it will read (\"Keep 412 photos from “Trip” on this Mac? 11.6 GB will be read from megham, in the background.\"), then reads the originals one at a time into the cache of originals, with progress and Cancel in the status bar. Photos already in the cache are kept at once without reading them again, and the photo you're editing always reads first. Kept photos open in Develop at full quality while the share is off, and they're never let go to keep the cache within its size (they don't count toward it). Settings › Storage shows their total as plus 11.6 GB kept. Atelier always leaves at least 10% of this Mac's disk free. If keeping would leave less, it says how much space to free first and reads nothing. Cancel stops reading; the photos already read stay kept. Free Up Space on the same menu removes the kept copies. The originals stay on the share and are read again when you open them. Only photos on a network share are kept. For a cloud drive folder, use the cloud drive's own Keep Downloaded or Make available offline in Finder."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"changed","title":"When the original changed","page":"Storage locations","text":"If a photo's file on the share isn't the one you imported (another app saved over it, or a camera card was copied again), Atelier never quietly uses either file. It stops for that photo and asks: In Develop, a banner over the photo says NZ6_9144.NEF changed on megham since it was imported, with Use the New File and, for a photo you kept with Keep on This Mac, Use the Kept Copy. The preview stays on screen meanwhile. Export and Edit In carry on with the other photos, then show one sheet listing the changed ones. Pick Use the New File, Use the Kept Copy or Skip for each, and Continue runs the export (or Edit In) on the ones you let through. Skip All leaves them. Use the New File reads the new file once, with progress and Cancel in the status bar; the catalog takes it from then on, the previews are made again, and your edits stay. A kept photo stays kept, now with the new file. Use the Kept Copy works from the copy on this Mac from before the change, for this session; the photo still counts as changed, so Atelier asks again next time. AI recipes run on the photo open in Develop, so its banner is where you choose. When the share is offline, an export from a copy on this Mac still runs at full quality, and Show Problems in the status bar lists Export notes naming each photo whose copy couldn't be checked against the share."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"smart","title":"Smart previews","page":"Storage locations","text":"A smart preview is a small editable copy of a photo (a 2560 px DNG of about 1 MB) that Atelier keeps on this Mac, so you can go on editing in Develop while the original is offline, as in Lightroom. They're made in the background, with progress and Cancel in the status bar, behind the photo you're opening. The first photo of each camera model takes a few seconds longer, while Atelier learns how that camera's white balance works. By themselves: a location with Make smart previews for photos here switched on (in Add Location…, or in its details under Settings › Storage) gets one for every photo you import into it. It's on to start for network shares and cloud drive folders and off to start for drives and folders, which you can switch on too. For any photos: select photos whose originals are online, then choose Library › Previews › Build Smart Previews. Under the Library's histogram, Atelier says what it can edit the photo from: Original Photo, Original + Smart Preview, Smart Preview (the original is offline) or Photo is missing. When the original is offline and the photo has no copy in the cache of originals, Develop opens its smart preview and says Smart Preview under the histogram. Every edit works as usual and is saved in the catalog; your previews update, and the edits apply to the original when it's back. When its drive or share reconnects while you're editing, Develop switches to the original by itself, with your edits as they are. Temp, Tint and Exposure behave as they do"},{"file":"storage.html","id":"offline","title":"When a location is offline","page":"Storage locations","text":"Its photos stay in the grid from their previews, with the offline mark, and you can still rate, flag and keyword them. One with a copy in the cache of originals or a smart preview also opens in Develop. Library › Refresh Volumes (⌘R) checks again after you connect it some other way."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"backups","title":"Back up the catalog","page":"Storage locations","text":"Atelier keeps the last few copies of the catalog inside it each time it quits (Settings › General). To keep copies somewhere else as well, choose a location in Settings › Storage › Backups: Back up the catalog to: any location: a drive, your NAS, a cloud drive folder or a bucket. Backups go to its Atelier Backups folder, one file per backup, such as catalog-2026-11-17T213000.sqlite (the time is UTC). When: every time Atelier quits, once a day or once a week, at quit. A small window shows the progress; Skip This Time quits at once. Keep: how many backups stay there. The oldest are removed; nothing else in the folder is touched. Back Up Now, or File › Back Up Catalog, makes one straight away, in the background with progress and Cancel in the status bar. The last backup's time and size, or what went wrong, show under the choices. To restore a backup, quit Atelier and copy it over catalog.sqlite inside the catalog (right-click the catalog in Finder › Show Package Contents). Photos and previews aren't in the catalog backup: back the photos up with your drives."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"s3","title":"Object storage (S3)","page":"Storage locations","text":"A bucket on Amazon S3, Backblaze B2, Cloudflare R2, Wasabi or MinIO can hold catalog backups. Photos stay on drives, shares and cloud drive folders. In Settings › Storage click +, choose Object Storage (S3) and Continue. Choose the Service. Atelier fills the endpoint from the region; for Cloudflare R2 type your account's endpoint, for MinIO its address, such as http://localhost:9000. Enter the Bucket, an optional Folder inside it, and the Access Key and Secret Key. Use keys that can only reach this bucket. Click Test Connection (or Continue, which tests first). A tick means Atelier can list the bucket; otherwise it says what's wrong, such as a secret key that doesn't match or a bucket in another region. Check the name and click Add Location. The first bucket you add becomes the backup location. The keys are kept in your Keychain as Atelier: <name>, never in the catalog, its backups or anything a chat app can see. In the bucket's details, Test Connection checks it again and Sign In Again… replaces the keys. Removing the bucket removes its keys from the Keychain; the backups in it stay."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"where","title":"What stays on this Mac","page":"Storage locations","text":"The catalog, every preview and the smart previews always stay on this Mac (smart previews inside the catalog, in its Smart Previews folder), whatever locations you add, so browsing never waits on the network and a sync app can never damage the catalog."},{"file":"storage.html","id":"remove","title":"Remove a location","page":"Storage locations","text":"Select it and click −, or Remove Location… in its details. Its photos stay in the catalog (offline while the drive isn't there), and nothing on the location is changed. See also: Import photos · Drives and the NAS · Settings"},{"file":"develop.html","id":"","title":"Develop basics","page":"Develop","text":"Develop is where you edit a photo. Every change is a setting stored in the catalog, rendered live from the original: the original file is never changed, and every step can be undone. The Develop viewZoom and move aroundNavigator Before and afterPanelsHistory and undo KeyboardSnapshotsCopy and paste settingsHistogram"},{"file":"develop.html","id":"layout","title":"The Develop view","page":"Develop basics","text":"Select a photo and press D, click Develop in the toolbar, or right-click a photo › Edit in Develop. ImportExport GridLoupeDevelop Navigator Presets › Atelier5› Film6› Black & White6 Snapshots Warm version History Exposure +0.45Profile: Camera MatchingImport Before and After OffSide by SideSlider FitFill100%200% Histogram ▭ ◌ ✧ ⌄ Basic Exposure+0.45Contrast+20Highlights−45 › Tone Curve› HSL / Color› Color Grading› Detail All Photographs · 5 photos · 1 of 5 · DSC_0004.NEF ⌄ Navigator: the whole photo; shows where you are when zoomed and previews presets. Presets and Snapshots. Click a section's header band to fold it. History: every change you made, newest first. The photo, rendered live. Before and After and the zoom buttons. The tool strip: Crop & Straighten, Remove, Masking, AI and Lens Blur, which opens its own mode. The adjustment panels, from Basic to Calibration. The Filmstrip: the other photos in the list. Arrow keys move between them."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"zoom","title":"Zoom and move around","page":"Develop basics","text":"Double-click the photo, or ZSwitch between Fit and 100% at the spot you clicked. A single click does nothing, so a stray click never loses your zoom. Fit · Fill · 100% · 200%The buttons under the photo. Fit (arrows pointing in) shows the whole photo; Fill (arrows pointing out) fills the space. Rest the pointer on a button for its name. Pinch, or ⌥/⌘ + scrollZoom freely, around the pointer. Drag, scroll, or hold Space and dragMove around while zoomed. ⌘= · ⌘- · ⌘0 · ⌥⌘1Zoom in, zoom out, Fit, 100% (View menu). The zoom and the spot you're looking at stay put when you open a tool (Crop, Masking, Guided Upright, the color pickers) and come back. Atelier › Settings… › Develop chooses what a double-click zooms to (50% to 400%) and what happens when you move to another photo: open it at Fit or Fill, return to where you left that photo, or keep the same zoom as in Lightroom."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"navigator","title":"Navigator","page":"Develop basics","text":"The small photo at the top of the left column. When you're zoomed in, a white rectangle shows the part in view. Drag the rectangle to move around the photo. Click anywhere else in the Navigator to jump there. At Fit, a click zooms in on that spot. Rest the pointer on a preset to preview it here, without changing the photo."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"before-after","title":"Before and after","page":"Develop basics","text":"Compare your edit with the original. When you move through photos in either layout, Before follows the photo you stop on. While it loads, the Before side is blank with a spinner. When zoomed in, the images appear first and sharpen when the full-resolution versions are ready. \\Show the original until you press it again (the Before button under the panels does the same). Side by SideBefore on the left, after on the right. Both zoom and pan together. SliderOne photo with a divider: original on the left, your edit on the right. Drag the divider to compare; drag the photo to move around when zoomed. YStep through Off, Side by Side and Slider. Like this. The Slider view works like this picture: drag across it. The three buttons under the photo are icons (Off, Side by Side, Slider); rest the pointer on one for its name and key. A compare view opens at Fit. Going back to Off returns you to the zoom you had. As the photo column narrows, the hint goes first and then the zoom buttons fold into a menu."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"panels","title":"Panels","page":"Develop basics","text":"Click a header band to open or fold a panel. Atelier remembers which are open. The left column's sections (Navigator, Presets, Snapshots, History) work the same way. A blue dot next to the name means the panel has edits; ↺ resets just that panel. The on/off switch (every panel but Basic) turns the whole panel off to compare, keeping its settings. While it's off the controls are dimmed but still work: moving one turns the panel back on. Exports and presets follow the switch. Right-click a header for Solo Mode (opening one panel closes the others), Expand All Panels, Collapse All Panels and that panel's Help page. Drag the panel's left edge to make the column wider or narrower."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"group-reset","title":"Reset a group","page":"Develop basics","text":"Double-click a group heading inside a panel (White Balance, Tone, Presence, Region, Sharpening, Grain…) to reset just those sliders. Or hold ⌥: the heading reads “Reset Tone” and one click resets it, as in Lightroom."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"sliders","title":"Sliders","page":"Develop basics","text":"Double-click a slider's name to reset it. Shift-double-click a Tone slider's name to set it automatically. Click the value to type a number; Return sets it, Esc cancels. Bold means changed. Over the knob the pointer becomes a left-right arrow ↔: press anywhere near it and drag, and the knob moves from where it is, without jumping. Click elsewhere on the track to jump the knob there, then keep dragging. The arrow stays for the whole drag. Every slider in Atelier looks and works this way: Develop and Masking, Crop and the AI brush, the Presets Amount, Export, Cull's Grouping, Match Look's Strength, the grid's thumbnail size and the HDR app. The two-ended Defringe hue sliders use the same knobs and arrow. The small tick on the track is the default; the blue fill is your change. Colored tracks (Temp, Tint, Vibrance, HSL) show which way each end goes. Hold ⌥ while dragging many sliders to see what they affect: see ⌥-drag views."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"keyboard","title":"Keyboard","page":"Develop basics","text":"Develop works from the keyboard the Mac way. Whatever has keyboard focus shows a thin blue bar at its left edge and a faint tint. Click, drag or Tab to a sliderGives it focus. ← → ↑ ↓ move it a small step; hold ⇧ for five times as much. Holding a key keeps it moving; quick presses in a row make one history step. Tab · ⇧TabNext or previous control in the area that has focus, down through the open panels (wraps around). Panel headers are stops too: Return or Space opens or closes the panel, → opens it, ← closes it. After ⌃Tab reaches an area, Tab walks its controls and stays in that area (in Presets it also stops at the search field). With nothing focused, Tab starts in the right panels. Click in an areaGives that area the keyboard, as ⌃Tab does: anywhere in a panel focuses the control you used last there (or the top one); the photo or Filmstrip gives ← → back to changing photos. ⌃Tab · ⌃⇧TabNext or previous area: the left column (Presets), the right panels, then the photos (where ← → change photo). A blue ring with a soft glow flashes around the area you land on (for the photos, around the Filmstrip when it is showing), and focus comes back to the control you used last there. Esc, or click the photoGives the arrow keys back to the photo, so ← → move between photos again. The Presets list takes the keys too: see Presets from the keyboard. Change ⌃Tab and the other command keys in Atelier › Settings… › Keyboard."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"color-pickers","title":"Color pickers","page":"Develop basics","text":"The White Balance Selector, Defringe's Fringe Color Selector and Color Grading's color picker all work the same way: click the eyedropper, then click the photo. A hint at the top of the photo says what to click (in orange if the click didn't set anything, with the reason). You can still zoom and move around while it's out, only one is out at a time, and Esc puts it away."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"history","title":"History and undo","page":"Develop basics","text":"Every change is a named step in History (Exposure +0.45, Straighten 4.0°, Preset: Punchy). History is kept with the photo. A drag records one step when you release the mouse, even if you pause partway through. ⌘Z undoes and ⇧⌘Z redoes, one step at a time. Click a step to go back to it. A new change from there replaces the steps after it. The ✕ in the History header clears the list but keeps the current look."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"snapshots","title":"Snapshots","page":"Develop basics","text":"Save a look you like by name, keep editing, and come back to it. Click + in the Snapshots header and type a name. Click a snapshot to apply it again (that's a history step too). Right-click a snapshot to Update with Current Settings, Rename… or Delete. Two versions side by side in the grid? Use a virtual copy instead: each copy has its own edits."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"copy-paste","title":"Copy, paste and reset","page":"Develop basics","text":"⇧⌘C or CopyCopy this photo's settings. ⇧⌘V or PastePaste them onto the selected photos. Right-click › Develop Settings works on the whole selection. ⇧⌘R or ResetClear every edit on this photo (one undoable step)."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"histogram","title":"Histogram","page":"Develop basics","text":"The graph at the top of the right column shows how much of the photo is at each brightness, from black on the left to white on the right, for red, green and blue. Where channels overlap they mix, as in Lightroom: yellow, cyan or magenta where two meet, gray where all three do. It's drawn from the edited photo and updates as you work, in step with the photo during a drag. ›Histogram ISO 40050 mmƒ/2.81/250 sec Under the graph: the ISO, focal length, aperture and shutter speed the photo was taken with. Hover over them for the camera, lens and exposure compensation. Clipping triangles. The small triangles in the top corners light up when a color channel reaches pure black (left) or pure white (right), in the color of those channels; white means all three. Click a triangle to see on the photo what is really lost: pure white turns red and pure black turns blue. Saturated colors with only one channel at the end, like a deep blue sky, keep their detail and are not marked. J or Develop › Show Clipping turns both on or off. The colors are only on screen, never in exports. How much is clipped. Hover over a triangle to read the share of the photo it counts. Settings › Develop › Panels can show it next to the triangle as well, from half a percent. Drag to adjust. Hover over the graph and the part under the pointer lights up: Blacks, Shadows, Exposure, Highlights or Whites, from left to right. The line underneath shows that slider and its value. Drag left or right to change it, or double-c"},{"file":"develop.html","id":"frame-diagnostics","title":"Frame diagnostics","page":"Develop basics","text":"In the single-photo Develop view or a Before/After compare view, choose Develop › Frame Diagnostics and drag a slider, Tone Curve point or Color Grading wheel. The overlay estimates how many new photo frames reach a display refresh each second. It shows time spent handling a gesture tick on the main thread, rendering, and waiting for a refresh after the frame is ready, plus the longest gap between photo frames during the drag. 92 fps / 120 Hz · estimated displayMain tick 1.0 msMain pass 4.2 / 12.8 msRender 7.3 msReady → refresh 5.6 msLongest gap 25.0 msCG fallbacks 2 Illustrative values. Refresh timing is an estimate. Main tick covers the gesture callback. Main pass shows mean and worst busy time per main run-loop pass, including deferred SwiftUI updates and other work on that thread. CG fallbacks counts ordinary images generated because the surface pool was full or live frames were turned off during the drag. Atelier writes a summary each active second to Documents/Atelier Traces, including the starting value, time to the first changed frame and longest gap for each completed drag, how many main-thread passes took longer than one display refresh, and how many histogram updates the drag caused. In Instruments, each main-thread pass during a drag shows as a Main pass interval under Points of Interest. The same log notes, for each photo you open, how many tool pipelines were prepared in the background before your first drag and how long that took, and how long each save of your"},{"file":"basic.html","id":"","title":"Basic","page":"Develop","text":"The panel you'll use on almost every photo: color or black and white, the starting look, white balance, brightness and contrast, and the presence sliders that add depth and color. TreatmentProfileWhite Balance ExposureContrastHighlights & Shadows Whites & BlacksAutoTexture & Clarity DehazeVibrance & Saturation⌥-drag views ›Basic↺ TreatmentColorBlack & White ProfileCamera Matching White Balance WBAs Shot✎ ToneAuto Presence Treatment: Color or Black & White (V). Profile: the starting look the sliders build on. White Balance: the WB menu, the eyedropper, Temp and Tint. Tone: brightness, contrast and the four range sliders, with Auto. A changed slider shows its change filled in blue from the small mark (the default) and its value in bold. Presence: Texture, Clarity, Dehaze, Vibrance and Saturation. The dot means this panel has edits. Click ↺ to reset just this panel. Basic is the one panel without an on/off switch. Every slider works the same way. Drag the knob, or click the number on the right and type a value. Double-click the slider's name to reset it. Double-click a group heading (Tone, Presence) to reset the group. Click a panel's header to fold it away; right-click a header for Solo Mode, which keeps one panel open at a time."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"treatment","title":"Treatment: Color or Black & White","page":"Basic","text":"Switches the photo between color and black and white. In Black & White, the B&W Mix replaces HSL, so you can choose how bright each color becomes in gray. Press V to switch. Drag across the picture to compare. Color Grading still works in Black & White, so you can tone the gray (Sepia, Selenium)."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"profile","title":"Profile","page":"Basic","text":"The look your photo starts from, under every slider. Only RAW files have a choice; for JPEG and HEIC the menu is dimmed. Camera MatchingThe default. Matches the preview your camera saved in the RAW file, including its Picture Control and Active D-Lighting, so the photo looks like it did on the camera's screen. Apple NeutralApple's plain RAW render: flatter, with more room in the highlights. A good start for heavy edits."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"white-balance","title":"White Balance","page":"Basic","text":"Makes whites white. Fix it first: every color after it depends on it."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"wb-menu","title":"The WB menu","page":"Basic","text":"As ShotWhat the camera chose. AutoAtelier makes the photo's nearly gray colors neutral. Also Develop › Auto White Balance (⇧⌘U). Daylight, Cloudy, Shade, Tungsten, Fluorescent, FlashLightroom's presets for that light (RAW files only). CustomShown once you move Temp or Tint yourself."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"wb-selector","title":"White Balance Selector","page":"Basic","text":"Click the eyedropper next to the WB menu, or press W. The pointer becomes an eyedropper. Click something in the photo that should be neutral gray or white: a cloud, a gray rock, a white shirt. Temp and Tint are set so that spot has no color cast, and the eyedropper goes away. Esc cancels."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"temp-tint","title":"Temp and Tint","page":"Basic","text":"Temp runs from blue to yellow and Tint from green to magenta; the colored tracks show which way is which. For RAW files Temp is in Kelvin (2,000 to 50,000 K). JPEG, HEIC and TIFF files have no real Kelvin, so Temp and Tint go from −100 to +100, with 0 as shot. Try it. Drag the slider. Moving right makes the photo warmer. Shortcut. Shift-double-click the name Temp or Tint for Auto white balance."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"exposure","title":"Exposure","page":"Basic","text":"Overall brightness, in stops: +1.00 is twice as bright. On JPEG, HEIC and TIFF files the brightest tones roll off softly toward white instead of clipping. Try it. Set the brightness of the subject first; fix the brightest and darkest parts with Highlights and Shadows after."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"contrast","title":"Contrast","page":"Basic","text":"Moves bright tones up and dark tones down (or together, when negative). Each color keeps its hue: an orange gets brighter or darker but never turns red or yellow."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"highlights-shadows","title":"Highlights and Shadows","page":"Basic","text":"Recover a bright sky or open up dark areas without touching the rest. They work on whole areas, not single pixels, so a sky or a shadow moves together and its detail stays. Highlights below 0 brings back the bright light rays. Shadows above 0 opens up the dark lagoon and the trees around the palace."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"whites-blacks","title":"Whites and Blacks","page":"Basic","text":"Set the very brightest and darkest points. Whites up and Blacks down give a photo a full range from black to white; watch the histogram's clipping triangles (or press J to see clipping on the photo), or hold ⌥ while dragging (see ⌥-drag views)."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"auto","title":"Auto","page":"Basic","text":"The Auto button next to the Tone heading sets Exposure, Highlights, Shadows, Whites and Blacks from the photo. Contrast is left alone. It's one history step (Auto Tone), so ⌘Z undoes it. Just one slider. Shift-double-click a Tone slider's name to set only that slider automatically."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"texture-clarity","title":"Texture and Clarity","page":"Basic","text":"Both add or soften local contrast, on brightness only, so colors don't shift. Negative values smooth without blurring edges. TextureMedium-sized detail: feathers, skin, bark, foliage. Positive brings it out; negative softens skin. ClarityWider areas, mostly in the midtones: adds punch and depth. Negative gives a soft glow. Texture brings out each feather. Clarity adds depth to the whole scene."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"dehaze","title":"Dehaze","page":"Basic","text":"Removes haze, fog and mist (positive) or adds them (negative). It estimates how much haze lies over each area, so clear dark areas and the sky barely change."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"vibrance-saturation","title":"Vibrance and Saturation","page":"Basic","text":"Saturation makes every color stronger by the same amount. Vibrance boosts muted colors most and leaves already strong ones alone, so it's gentler on skin and less likely to look garish. In Black & White neither has any effect. Before Vibrance +60 Saturation +60"},{"file":"basic.html","id":"option-drag","title":"⌥-drag views: see what a slider does","page":"Basic","text":"Hold ⌥ while you press or drag these sliders. The photo is replaced by a view that shows exactly what the slider is changing, until you let go. Exposure, Highlights, WhitesBlack, with clipped highlights shown in the color of the channel that clips (white where all three clip). Drag until only what should be pure white shows. Shadows, BlacksWhite, with clipped shadows shown (black where all three clip). Detail, Color Grading and Defringe have ⌥ views too; each slider's tooltip says what it shows. See also: Develop basics · Tone Curve · HSL / Color and B&W · Presets"},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"","title":"Tone Curve","page":"Develop","text":"Reshape brightness exactly where you want it. The curve maps each tone of the photo (along the bottom) to a new brightness (up the side). PresetsParametric curvePoint curveCommon shapesRGB, Red, Green, BlueRefine SaturationTargeted Adjustment"},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"presets","title":"Tone Curve presets","page":"Tone Curve","text":"The Preset menu at the top of the panel sets the whole curve at once: the Parametric regions and splits, the RGB, Red, Green and Blue curves and Refine Saturation. Nothing else on the photo changes, so it works on top of any Basic, HSL or Color Grading setting. The menu shows the name of the preset the curve matches, or Custom once you change it. ›Tone Curve↺ PresetCross Process Basic Linear Medium Contrast Strong Contrast Lighter Darker Film Process ✓ Cross Process Reverse Cross Process Lomo Expired Film Instant Film Cinematic Teal & Orange Matrix Green Neon Night Day for Night Fade Matte Faded Film Vintage Fade Effects Negative Color Negative Solarize User Presets My Portrait Curve Save Preset… Delete Preset › Preview: open the menu and rest the pointer on a preset. The Navigator (top left) shows the photo with that curve; the photo itself doesn't change until you click. Apply: click it. The panel switches to the Point view on the curve the preset bends (with Show All Curves on when it bends Red, Green and Blue), so you see what it did. It is one step in History (“Tone Curve Preset: Cross Process”), so ⌘Z undoes it. Save your own: shape the curve, then Preset › Save Preset… and name it. It is listed under User Presets on every photo and catalog. Saving under a name you already used replaces that preset. Delete Preset removes one. Linear clears the whole curve. Basic has Lightroom's Medium and Strong Contrast points. The rest are looks, most made with the Red, Green and Blue"},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"parametric","title":"Parametric curve","page":"Tone Curve","text":"The quick way to shape tones without placing points. Pick Parametric at the top of the panel. ›Tone Curve↺ PresetCustom ◎ParametricPoint ▲▲▲ Region Targeted Adjustment (◎), then Parametric or Point. The curve over the histogram. Drag up or down on it to move the region under the pointer, which is shaded. Split points: drag a triangle to move where one region ends and the next begins. Right-click the graph to reset regions or splits. HighlightsThe brightest tones, above the right triangle. LightsBetween the middle and right triangles. DarksBetween the left and middle triangles. ShadowsThe darkest tones, below the left triangle. Each goes from −100 to +100. Black and white never move, so Shadows +100 opens up dark areas without turning black gray. Lights up and Darks down is a gentle S-curve. The region curve works on top of Basic tone, as in Lightroom."},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"curve","title":"Point curve","page":"Tone Curve","text":"›Tone Curve↺ PresetCustom RGBRedGreenBlue⋯ Input 64 Output 46 Channel: RGB changes brightness; Red, Green and Blue change one color each. The ⋯ menu (also on right-click) has Linear, Medium Contrast and Strong Contrast, your saved curves, Save Curve…, Copy and Paste a channel, Reset, Snap to Grid and Show All Curves. The curve, over the photo's histogram. The dashed diagonal is “no change”. Left is black, right is white. Input and Output of the selected point, 0 to 255. Type a number to move it. Add a point: click anywhere on the curve, then drag it. Up brightens those tones, down darkens them. A curve holds up to 16 points. Move a point: drag it, or with the pointer over the graph press the arrow keys (⇧ for bigger steps). Points stay in order from left to right. Remove a point: drag it off the graph, or double-click it. The two end points always stay."},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"shapes","title":"Common shapes","page":"Tone Curve","text":"S-curve (like the one drawn above, or Medium Contrast in the ⋯ menu): darks down, lights up. More contrast in the midtones. Matte: raise the left end point so black becomes dark gray, and lower the right end a little. A faded film look. Basic tone, the region curve and the RGB curve keep each color's hue, as Lightroom does: a curve makes colors brighter or darker, never a different color. The faint second line under the RGB curve shows the combined result of Basic tone and the region curve."},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"channels","title":"Red, Green and Blue curves","page":"Tone Curve","text":"These work on one channel at a time, so they do shift color, on purpose. Raise the Blue curve's left end to put blue into the shadows; lower its right end for yellow highlights. Turn on Show All Curves to see the other channels faintly. For tinting by tone, Color Grading is usually easier."},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"refine","title":"Refine Saturation","page":"Tone Curve","text":"Shown under the Point curve once the RGB curve is bent. A steep curve makes colors more saturated as well as more contrasty, as in Lightroom (100, the default). Lower it to keep the contrast but hold back the extra saturation; at 0 the curve changes brightness only."},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"targeted","title":"Targeted Adjustment","page":"Tone Curve","text":"Click ◎ at the top of the panel, then drag up or down on the photo over the tone you want to change. In Parametric it moves the region sliders for that tone; in Point it adds a point for that tone on the channel shown (or moves the one already there). Each drag is one step in History. Click ◎ again or press Esc to put it away. See also: Contrast · Histogram · Color Grading"},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"","title":"HSL / Color and B&W Mix","page":"Develop","text":"Change one color without touching the others: make greens more golden, calm down orange skin, darken a blue sky. Point Color does the same for one exact color you pick. In Black & White, the same panel decides how bright each color becomes in gray. The eight colorsHueSaturationLuminanceTargeted AdjustmentPoint ColorB&W Mix"},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"bands","title":"The eight colors","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"Pick Mixer at the top of the panel. Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Aqua, Blue, Purple and Magenta each have three sliders. Neighboring colors blend smoothly, and grays, whites and the faint color noise in dark areas are never affected. ›HSL / Color↺ MixerPoint Color ◎HueSaturationLuminanceAll Targeted Adjustment (◎), then show Hue, Saturation or Luminance one at a time, or All 24 sliders. Each track is painted with the colors that end of the slider gives, so you can see which way to drag."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"hue","title":"Hue","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"Shifts a color toward its neighbors. Green toward yellow gives autumn foliage; blue toward aqua gives tropical water."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"saturation","title":"Saturation","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"Makes one color stronger or weaker. Turning down Orange and Red calms skin and wood; turning up Blue deepens a sky. At −100 a color turns gray at the brightness you saw it, so a deep blue sky becomes a dark gray, not a pale one."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"luminance","title":"Luminance","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"Makes one color brighter or darker. Darkening Blue makes skies dramatic; lightening Orange brightens faces."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"targeted","title":"Targeted Adjustment","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"Click ◎ next to the channel buttons, then drag up or down on the photo over the color you want to change: the sliders for that color move together (a sky moves Blue and a little Aqua, say). In All, ◎ is a menu: choose Hue, Saturation or Luminance first. Each drag is one step in History; Esc puts the tool away. It works the same way in Tone Curve and the B&W Mix."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"point-color","title":"Point Color","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"For one exact color rather than a whole band: a skin tone, a jacket, the green of one plant. ›HSL / Color↺ MixerPoint Color ✎ Point Color 1 Range ☐ Visualize Range Eyedropper and swatches: click the eyedropper, then the color in the photo. Up to four swatches; click one to edit it, right-click it to delete it. Range widens or narrows which colors count as “this color”. Uniformity pulls them toward the picked color, evening out blotchy skin or a patchy sky (beyond Lightroom, after Capture One's skin tone tool). Visualize Range shows only the colors the swatch changes; the rest of the photo turns gray. HueTurns the picked color toward its neighbors, up to 30°. SaturationMakes it stronger or weaker. LuminanceMakes it brighter or darker."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"bw-mix","title":"B&W Mix","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"When Treatment is Black & White (press V), this panel shows the B&W Mix instead of HSL: eight sliders that set how light or dark each color becomes in gray, like colored filters on black and white film. B&W Mix at 0 Green +90, Yellow +80, Orange −60 The same conversion with the green and yellow grass lifted, so the hills glow against the dark oaks. Classic filters. Darken Blue for a dramatic sky (a red filter). Lighten Orange and Red for smooth skin. Lighten Green and Yellow for bright foliage. ◎ works here too: drag up or down on a color in the photo to make its gray lighter or darker. Vibrance and Saturation have no effect in Black & White. Color Grading still works, for toned prints (Sepia, Selenium in the Black & White presets). See also: Vibrance and Saturation · Color Grading"},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"","title":"Color Grading","page":"Develop","text":"Tint the shadows, midtones and highlights separately: teal shadows and warm highlights for a film look, a cool cast for night, a sepia tone for black and white. PresetsColor harmonyReading a wheelDragging3-Way and single wheels Blending and BalanceColor pickerCompare and soloRight-click menu Shadows: Hue 195, Saturation 55. Highlights: Hue 35, Saturation 55. Drag across to compare."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"presets","title":"Color Grading presets","page":"Color Grading","text":"The Preset menu at the top of the panel sets every wheel (Shadows, Midtones, Highlights and Global) plus Blending and Balance at once. Nothing else on the photo changes. The menu shows the preset the panel matches, or Custom. ›Color Grading↺ PresetTeal & Orange Complementary None ✓ Teal & Orange Blockbuster Neon Noir Desert Heat Split Complementary Coral Reef Sunset Split Analogous Golden Hour Moonlight Matrix Green Arctic Autumn Triad Psychedelic Technicolor Monochrome Toning Sepia Cyanotype Selenium Gold Tone Save Preset… Preview: open the menu and rest the pointer on a preset; the Navigator shows the photo with it. Apply: click it (one step in History). Then fine-tune the wheels as usual; the menu reads Custom. Save your own: Preset › Save Preset…. Your presets are listed under User Presets; Delete Preset removes one. None clears all Color Grading. The built-ins are strong on purpose (Saturation up to 100 on the main wheel), because a grade only tints: lower a wheel's Saturation to tone one down. Monochrome Toning is made for Black & White photos. For a full look, pair one with a Tone Curve preset, for example Teal & Orange on both."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"harmony","title":"Color harmony: what the groups mean","page":"Color Grading","text":"The groups are named after where their hues sit on the color wheel, the patterns painters and designers call color harmonies. ComplementaryTwo hues opposite each other (teal and orange, blue and gold). The strongest contrast between warm and cool. Split complementaryOne hue plus the two either side of its opposite: softer than a straight complement. AnalogousNeighboring hues (orange to yellow, teal to blue). Calm and unified; the most natural-looking. TriadThree hues evenly spaced, a triangle on the wheel. The boldest: Psychedelic and Technicolor put one hue in each tonal range. MonochromeOne hue through every tone: classic toning such as sepia or cyanotype. These are traditions from art and design, not rules of how we see. Studies of people's ratings find that colors with similar hues are judged most harmonious, and that complementary pairs are liked for their contrast rather than for harmony. Warm hues sit in the highlights or midtones in these presets because skin is orange; that keeps faces natural, which is why teal-and-orange works so often. Beyond Lightroom. Lightroom has no Color Grading presets of its own; you save a Develop preset with only Color Grading ticked. That still works here (Presets), and is the way to save a grade together with a tone curve or HSL."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"wheel","title":"Reading a wheel","page":"Color Grading","text":"Each wheel is a circle of colors. The angle picks the hue (red at 3 o'clock, then yellow, green, cyan, blue and magenta going counterclockwise) and the distance from the center is how strong the tint is. The center is no tint at all. 1 2 3 4 5 The inner dot (hollow, filled with the tint it gives) sets hue and saturation together. Drag it. The rim dot sets the hue only. Drag it around the edge to change the color but keep the strength. The spoke runs from the center through the dot to the rim, so you can see the direction of the tint. The faint black dots are the colors in your photo that fall in this wheel's range (here, the shadows), like a small vectorscope. A cluster away from the center shows a color cast; as you move the inner dot, the cluster moves with it. The small ring is where another wheel's tint is (here, the warm Highlights tint), so you can line tints up or set them opposite. Beyond Lightroom. The photo's color dots and the other wheels' rings aren't in Lightroom. They update as you edit."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"dragging","title":"Dragging, with modifier keys","page":"Color Grading","text":"Drag the inner dotHue and saturation. It moves from where it is, so it never jumps under the pointer. Click elsewhere on the wheelPut the dot there. Drag along the spokeChanges only the strength: the hue holds until you move well off the line. ⇧ dragSaturation only. ⌘ dragHue only. ⌥ dragFine adjustment: moves at a tenth of the speed. Double-click inside a wheelReset its hue and saturation. Double-click a wheel's nameReset the whole wheel, Luminance included. ← → · ↑ ↓With the pointer over a wheel: turn the hue · change saturation. Add ⇧ for steps of 10."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"views","title":"3-Way and single wheels","page":"Color Grading","text":"The row of buttons at the top of the panel picks the view. Atelier remembers it. ›Color Grading↺ PresetCustom Adjust ⁂ |✎ Midtones 👁 H 0 · S 0 · L 0 Shadows 👁 H 195 · S 55 · L 0 Highlights 👁 H 35 · S 55 · L 0 Blend View buttons: 3-Way, then one large wheel at a time for Shadows, Midtones, Highlights or Global (all tones). Double-click Adjust to reset all Color Grading. Color picker (eyedropper): see Color picker. In 3-Way, each wheel has a Luminance slider under it and its values (H · S · L) below. A single-wheel view has Hue, Saturation and Luminance sliders instead, where you can type exact numbers. Blending and Balance, with a graph of where each range applies: see below. The panel switch turns all Color Grading off to compare, keeping the settings. Every panel but Basic has one. Luminance lightens or darkens that tonal range. It rolls off toward white and black instead of clipping: lightening the shadows lifts and tints the blacks, darkening the highlights tints the whites. Global tints the whole photo, on top of the three ranges."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"blend","title":"Blending and Balance","page":"Color Grading","text":"BlendingHow much the three ranges overlap. Low gives distinct bands; high (the default is 50) blends them smoothly. BalanceMoves the split between shadows and highlights. Negative gives the Shadows wheel more of the photo; positive gives Highlights more. The graph under the sliders shows the three ranges from black (left) to white (right): blue for Shadows, gray for Midtones and yellow for Highlights. It reshapes as you move the sliders. Hold ⌥ on Balance to see the photo painted in the three ranges' colors, so you can see exactly where the split falls. On Blending, ⌥ shows every wheel's tint at full strength. On a single wheel's Hue slider, ⌥ shows that tint at full strength."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"picker","title":"Color picker: remove a color cast","page":"Color Grading","text":"Click the eyedropper at the top right of the panel. The pointer becomes an eyedropper. Click a spot in the photo that should have no color: a gray wall, a white shirt in shadow. Atelier sets a wheel to the opposite tint, so that spot turns neutral. In 3-Way it picks Shadows, Midtones or Highlights by how bright the spot is; in a single-wheel view it sets that wheel. Esc cancels. Beyond Lightroom. Lightroom's Color Grading has no picker. Use White Balance first for an overall cast; use this for a cast in just the shadows or just the highlights."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"compare","title":"Compare and solo","page":"Color Grading","text":"The switch in the panel header turns Color Grading off and on. The settings are kept (and exports, presets and history follow the switch). Press and hold the eye 👁 next to a wheel's name to see the photo graded by that wheel alone."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"menu","title":"Right-click menu","page":"Color Grading","text":"Right-click a wheel for Reset Hue and Saturation, Reset Luminance, Reset (the whole wheel), Copy … Settings and Paste Settings to … (copy one wheel's tint to another), and Reset All Color Grading. See also: White Balance · HSL / Color and B&W · Presets (the Film and Cinematic packs use Color Grading)"},{"file":"detail.html","id":"","title":"Detail","page":"Develop","text":"Sharpen fine detail and clean up noise. Judge both at 100%: double-click the photo or press Z first. SharpeningSee what you're sharpeningNoise ReductionAI Denoise ›Detail↺ Sharpening Noise Reduction"},{"file":"detail.html","id":"sharpening","title":"Sharpening","page":"Detail","text":"Amount 0–150How much to sharpen. Start around 40 to 70. Radius 0.5–3.0How wide the sharpened edges are. Low for fine detail (feathers, landscapes), higher for soft subjects. Detail 0–100, default 25Below 25 holds back halos on strong edges; above 25 brings out fine texture. Masking 0–100Keeps sharpening to edges. 0 sharpens everywhere; 100 only the strongest edges, so skies and skin stay smooth. At 100%, sharpening crisps the finch’s feathers around the eye and the edge of the beak."},{"file":"detail.html","id":"sharpen-option","title":"See what you're sharpening (⌥)","page":"Detail","text":"Hold ⌥ while you drag a Sharpening slider. You can press or release ⌥ while holding the slider still: AmountThe photo in grayscale, so color doesn't distract you. Radius, DetailOnly the sharpening's edge halos, on mid gray. MaskingA black and white mask: white is sharpened, black is left alone. Drag until flat areas turn black."},{"file":"detail.html","id":"noise","title":"Noise Reduction","page":"Detail","text":"AI Denoise 0–100Takes the noise out of a RAW photo with a neural network built into Atelier, keeping fine detail far better than Luminance. See AI Denoise below. LuminanceSmooths grainy speckle in brightness (high ISO). Too much looks plasticky; stop when the grain is calm. ColorRemoves colored blotches in shadows and flat areas. A little goes a long way. Luminance noise reduction. Color noise reduction."},{"file":"detail.html","id":"ai-denoise","title":"AI Denoise","page":"Detail","text":"For high-ISO and underexposed RAW photos. Drag AI Denoise up from 0; 50 to 70 suits most photos, 100 takes out all the noise it finds. Its spread is measured on each photo, so it adjusts itself to the camera and ISO. The first time on a photo, the line under the slider says Finding the noise… for a few seconds while the photo is worked on at full size. The photo shows Atelier's usual noise reduction until it's done; after that every Amount is instant. While AI Denoise is above 0, the RAW engine's own noise reduction is off, so the slider starts from the photo's real noise. Use Luminance and Color on top if you want it smoother still. It works on RAW files only. On JPEG, TIFF and HEIC photos the slider does nothing and the line under it says so. Everything runs on your Mac (Core ML where it can), nothing is downloaded or sent anywhere, and the original stays untouched. Exports and previews wait for it. AI Denoise takes out speckle and color blotches while the doe’s fur and the leaves stay detailed. This is Atelier’s own model on a real ISO 2800 RAW, not an approximation. The model. PMRID (Wang et al., ECCV 2020, Apache License 2.0), about 4 MB, built into the app. Its results are fine for your website or shop. Order. Noise reduction runs before sharpening, so reduce noise first, then sharpen with some Masking so the noise isn't sharpened back. See also: Texture and Clarity · Grain"},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"","title":"Lens Corrections","page":"Develop","text":"Undo what the lens did: bent lines, dark corners and colored edges. Most of it is one checkbox. Remove Chromatic AberrationProfile correctionsDistortionDefringeVignetting ›Lens Corrections↺ Remove Chromatic Aberration Enable Profile Corrections NIKKOR Z 24-70mm f/4 S Distortion Defringe✎ Purple Hue 30/70 Green Hue 40/60 Vignetting"},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"chromatic-aberration","title":"Remove Chromatic Aberration","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"Removes red-cyan and blue-yellow edges that grow toward the corners. There are no sliders: Atelier measures how far the red and blue parts of the image are out of line in this photo and lines them up. Turn it on for most photos. A corner of the photo, enlarged: colored outlines on the trunks disappear."},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"profile-corrections","title":"Enable Profile Corrections","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"Fixes the lens's own distortion and dark corners using the correction data your camera writes into the RAW file (Nikon Z cameras do this). It's on by default for RAW files, and the panel shows the lens's name. When there's no data for a photo (or it isn't a RAW), the checkbox is dimmed with “No lens corrections for this photo”."},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"distortion","title":"Distortion","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"For anything the profile doesn't fix, or a lens with no profile. Positive removes barrel distortion (straight lines bowing outward); negative removes pincushion (lines bowing inward)."},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"defringe","title":"Defringe","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"Removes purple and green fringes along high-contrast edges, mostly from shooting at wide apertures. A fringe pixel loses its color but keeps its brightness. Branches against a bright sky, enlarged 3×. Purple Amount, Green Amount 0–20How much to remove. Higher reaches further from each edge. Purple Hue, Green HueWhich shades count as fringe, on a strip of the colors it covers. Drag an end to widen or narrow the range, or the middle to move it. Keep Green narrow so foliage is safe."},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"fringe-selector","title":"Fringe Color Selector","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"Zoom in on a fringe (double-click the photo). Click the eyedropper next to the Defringe heading. You can still zoom and move around. Click right on the purple or green fringe. Atelier centers that color's Hue range on it and turns its Amount up to 5 if it was 0. Anything else shows a message and the eyedropper stays out. Esc puts it away. Hold ⌥ while dragging: Amount shows white with only the fringes being removed, in their color; Hue shows black outside the range, so you can see which colors it covers. Only colors in a Hue range that sit near a strong edge change, so purple flowers or a green shirt away from edges keep their color."},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"vignetting","title":"Vignetting","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"Brightens (positive) or darkens the corners of the whole, uncropped photo, to undo the lens's dark corners. Midpoint sets how far in from the corners it reaches. For a creative vignette around a crop, use Effects › Post-Crop Vignetting. See also: Transform (perspective) · Crop & Straighten"},{"file":"transform.html","id":"","title":"Transform","page":"Develop","text":"Straighten leaning buildings, trees and artwork. Upright finds straight lines and fixes the perspective for you; Guided lets you draw the lines yourself. UprightGuidedManual slidersConstrain Crop Tilting the camera up makes vertical lines lean toward each other. Upright makes them parallel again."},{"file":"transform.html","id":"upright","title":"Upright","page":"Transform","text":"›Transform↺ OffAutoLevelVerticalFullGuided Constrain Crop OffNo Upright correction. AutoLike Full, but holds back when the lines are weak, so it never warps a photo hard on thin evidence. A safe first try. LevelMakes horizontal lines level. Rotation only. VerticalAlso makes vertical lines parallel. The usual choice for buildings and trees. FullAlso corrects horizontal perspective, for a wall shot from an angle. GuidedYou draw the lines. See Guided. If there aren't enough straight lines, the button says “Not enough straight lines found” and nothing changes. The correction is saved with the photo; click the mode again to measure again. Upright resets the crop. As in Lightroom, choosing an Upright mode resets the crop's cut and straighten angle and the manual Transform sliders (rotations and flips stay). ⌥-click the button to keep them."},{"file":"transform.html","id":"guided","title":"Guided","page":"Transform","text":"Click Guided or press ⇧T, then drag along edges that should be straight up or straight across. 1 2 3 Drag along a tree trunk or building edge to draw a guide. Each guide counts as vertical or horizontal, whichever it's closer to. The photo straightens once there are two guides, and updates with each guide you add or move (up to four). Two vertical guides fix leaning verticals; add horizontal guides to fix the horizontal perspective too. While you draw or move a guide's end, a 3× loupe beside the pointer shows exactly where you are. Show Loupe or O turns it off. Drag a guide's endMove it. Click a guide, then DeleteRemove it. Clear Guides removes all of them. ⌘⌥ drag the photoMove it with X and Y Offset (add ⇧ to move in one direction only). Esc or DoneClose Guided. A strong correction can push part of the photo out of the frame, guide ends included. Lower Scale to bring it back, or turn on Show Whole Photo to zoom the view out to the whole corrected photo. That changes only the view, never the edit. Beyond Lightroom. Show Whole Photo isn't in Lightroom, and every guide ends exactly straight even with four guides."},{"file":"transform.html","id":"sliders","title":"Manual sliders","page":"Transform","text":"Added on top of Upright, for fine-tuning or for doing it by hand. Vertical −100–100Negative widens the top: fixes buildings shot from below. Horizontal −100–100Turns the photo left or right, for a wall shot from the side. Rotate −10°–10°Small rotations. Aspect −100–100Positive stretches the width, negative the height. Fixes buildings that look too tall after a correction. Scale 50–150%Shrinks or enlarges the corrected photo in its frame. X Offset, Y OffsetMoves the photo sideways or up and down."},{"file":"transform.html","id":"constrain-crop","title":"Constrain Crop","page":"Transform","text":"Perspective corrections leave empty, light gray areas at the edges. Turn on Constrain Crop to crop to the largest rectangle with no gray, keeping your crop's shape. It finds the crop again whenever the correction changes, and the Crop tool won't let the crop leave the photo. Turning it off leaves the crop as it is. See also: Lens Corrections · Crop & Straighten"},{"file":"effects.html","id":"","title":"Effects","page":"Develop","text":"Finishing touches: a vignette that follows your crop, a glow from the highlights, and film grain. Post-Crop VignettingGlowGrain ›Effects↺ Post-Crop Vignetting StyleHighlight Priority ⌄ Glow StyleDiffusion ⌄ Grain TypeStandard ⌄"},{"file":"effects.html","id":"vignette","title":"Post-Crop Vignetting","page":"Effects","text":"StyleHighlight Priority (default) darkens like lower exposure but keeps bright areas bright. Color Priority keeps colors as they are while darkening. Paint Overlay mixes the edges toward black or white: flat and soft. AmountNegative darkens the edges and draws the eye in; positive lightens them for a soft, airy look. MidpointHow far toward the middle it reaches. Lower reaches further in. RoundnessAt 0 the vignette has the crop's proportions. Positive makes it rounder, toward a circle; negative squarer, toward a rounded rectangle. Feather default 50How soft the edge is. 0 gives a hard edge. HighlightsWith a negative Amount in Highlight or Color Priority, brings bright areas in the darkened corners back, such as a lamp or the sun. It's applied to the cropped photo, so it always frames what you kept. To fix a lens's dark corners instead, use Lens Corrections › Vignetting."},{"file":"effects.html","id":"glow","title":"Glow","page":"Effects","text":"Light spilling from the bright parts of the photo. Not in Lightroom Classic (Camera Raw and Luminar have one). StyleDiffusion: a soft, dreamy haze, like a diffusion filter. Bloom: highlights spill light onto what is around them. Halation: the red-orange glow film shows around bright lights, as on CineStill 800T. AmountHow strong the glow is. 0 is off. Range default 50Which tones glow. Higher lets darker tones glow too. Spread default 50How far the light reaches, as a share of the photo's size, so a preview and an export match. WarmthTints the glow warmer (positive) or cooler (negative)."},{"file":"effects.html","id":"grain","title":"Grain","page":"Effects","text":"Film-like grain. It changes brightness, not color, and is strongest in the midtones, so pure black and white stay clean. Type default StandardThe kind of film the grain comes from. Standard is Lightroom-like grain. Fine: the small, tight, crisp grain of slow film such as Ektar or Velvia. Cubic: the larger, sharp-edged, clumpy grain of classic film such as Tri-X, HP5 or Kodak Gold, reaching further into the shadows. Tabular: the smooth, soft, even grain of modern T-grain film such as Portra or T-Max, with cleaner shadows and highlights. Each type is equally strong at the same Amount. The built-in film presets pick the type that fits their film. AmountHow strong the grain is. Size default 25Grain particle size. Above 25 the photo is softened slightly, so large grain replaces fine detail as with real film. Roughness default 500 gives even, regular grain; higher clumps it and varies its strength across the frame. Shown enlarged 2×. Like Lightroom, grain is judged at 100%: at Fit, and in smaller exports, it looks weaker, as it would if the full-size photo were shrunk. See also: Noise Reduction · Film presets"},{"file":"calibration.html","id":"","title":"Calibration","page":"Develop","text":"Reshapes the red, green and blue the photo's colors are made of. One move shifts every color in the photo at once, so it's the place for a film-like palette or a camera whose colors you want to nudge in every shot. Red, Green and Blue PrimaryShadows TintWhere it works ›Calibration↺ Shadows Red Primary Green Primary Blue Primary"},{"file":"calibration.html","id":"primaries","title":"Red, Green and Blue Primary","page":"Calibration","text":"Every color in the photo is a mix of three primaries. Moving one primary moves every color that has some of it in the mix, which is why a small change here reaches further than the same change in HSL / Color. White, grays and black stay neutral whatever you do. HueTurns the primary toward its neighbours. Red moves toward orange (right) or magenta (left), green toward cyan (right) or yellow (left), blue toward purple (right) or cyan (left). The track shows where it goes. SaturationMakes everything that has this primary in it richer (right) or more muted (left). A well-known trick: Blue Primary Saturation up makes the whole photo richer and warmer, not just its blues, with fewer of the garish colors Saturation in Basic can give."},{"file":"calibration.html","id":"shadows-tint","title":"Shadows Tint","page":"Calibration","text":"Adds green (left) or magenta (right) to the shadows only, keeping their brightness. It's strongest in the deepest shadows and fades out before the midtones, so it can fix a camera that renders blacks a little green or magenta without changing the white balance of the rest of the photo."},{"file":"calibration.html","id":"order","title":"Where it works","page":"Calibration","text":"Calibration comes first among the color and tone panels, right after the profile and white balance, as in Lightroom. So Basic, HSL / Color and Color Grading all work on the reshaped colors, and Color Range masks still pick colors from the photo before it. Turn the panel off with the switch in its header to compare, as with any panel. New presets have a Calibration part, ticked by default; Copy and Paste Settings carry it too. Masks don't have Calibration sliders: it changes the whole photo."},{"file":"crop.html","id":"","title":"Crop & Straighten","page":"Tools","text":"Trim the edges, change the shape, level a horizon, rotate or flip. Nothing is cut from the original; you can change the crop any time. Open the toolOn the photoThe Crop panelStraighten"},{"file":"crop.html","id":"open","title":"Open the tool","page":"Crop & Straighten","text":"In Develop, press R or click the crop button (the first icon under the histogram). Press R, Return or click Done to close it. While it's open you see the whole photo with the crop on top."},{"file":"crop.html","id":"on-photo","title":"On the photo","page":"Crop & Straighten","text":"1 2 3 4 Corners: drag to resize from that corner. The pointer turns into a diagonal arrow. Edges: drag to move one side. Inside: drag to move the crop (the pointer is a hand). A rule-of-thirds grid helps you place the subject. Outside: drag to straighten (the pointer is a curved arrow). A finer grid appears while you rotate."},{"file":"crop.html","id":"panel","title":"The Crop panel","page":"Crop & Straighten","text":"›Crop & Straighten↺ Drag a corner or edge to crop, inside to move, outside to straighten. AspectOriginal⇄🔒 ↺↻⇋⇵ResetDone Aspect: Original, 1 × 1, 4 × 5 / 8 × 10, 5 × 7, 2 × 3, 3 × 4, 16 × 10, 16 × 9 or Free. The lock keeps the shape while you drag; unlock it to drag freely. ⇄ switches between landscape and portrait (or press X while cropping). Angle: straighten from −45° to 45°. Double-click the name to reset. Rotate left (⌘[), rotate right (⌘]), flip horizontal, flip vertical. Reset removes the crop, straightening, rotations and flips."},{"file":"crop.html","id":"straighten","title":"Straighten","page":"Crop & Straighten","text":"Drag outside the crop, or use the Angle slider. The photo turns smoothly under the frame, and the frame shrinks so it never shows past the photo's edge. Perspective, not just tilt? If verticals lean toward each other, use Transform › Upright instead of rotating. The crop applies everywhere: previews, the grid, exports, Edit In and AI. Masks stay on the same spot of the photo when you change the crop. See also: Transform · Zoom"},{"file":"remove.html","id":"","title":"Remove: Content-Aware, Heal and Clone","page":"Tools","text":"Take out sensor dust, specks, blemishes, wires and small distractions, filled from what's around them or from a source you choose. Each spot is a setting, not a change to the pixels: move it, resize it, pick another source or delete it any time. Open the toolMake a spotChange the sourceThe Remove panelVisualize SpotsKeys"},{"file":"remove.html","id":"open","title":"Open the tool","page":"Remove: Content-Aware, Heal and Clone","text":"In Develop, press Q, click the bandage button in the tool strip under the histogram (between Crop and Masking), or choose Develop › Remove Tool (Q). Press Q, Return or click Done to close it. Spots keep working after you close the tool; they're part of the photo's edit, in previews, exports and Edit In."},{"file":"remove.html","id":"spots","title":"Make a spot","page":"Remove: Content-Aware, Heal and Clone","text":"Click on a speck to make a round spot the size of the brush. Drag along a wire, a hair or a scratch to make a spot that follows the stroke. How the spot is filled depends on the mode at the top of the panel. Content-AwareThe default. Fills the spot from texture found all around it, piece by piece, so water, sky, grass or skin carry on through it, even where no one place nearby would do. The fill appears after a moment (the spot shows Heal until then) and is kept, so later edits don't redo it. Press / for another fill if the first one repeats something. HealAtelier picks a source next to the spot, the place where the photo around it looks most like the area around the spot. Heal copies the source's texture and blends it into the colors and brightness around the spot, so the fill meets the photo with no visible edge. Best for dust, skin and anything on a smooth or even area. CloneCopies the source exactly as it is. Use it where Heal would smear a pattern or an edge, for example along a line or next to a sharp border."},{"file":"remove.html","id":"source","title":"Change the source","page":"Remove: Content-Aware, Heal and Clone","text":"A selected Heal or Clone spot shows a solid outline, its source a dashed outline, with an arrow from the source to the spot. A Content-Aware spot has no source; switch it to Heal or Clone to choose one. Drag the dashed outline to take the fill from somewhere else. Press / for the next best source Atelier found (on a Content-Aware spot, another fill). Drag the solid outline to move the spot; its source moves with it. Click another spot to select it. Its settings show on the panel's sliders; changing them changes that spot. Press Delete to delete the selected spot, or Esc to deselect it. Every new spot, move, change and delete is one step in History, so ⌘Z undoes it."},{"file":"remove.html","id":"panel","title":"The Remove panel","page":"Remove: Content-Aware, Heal and Clone","text":"›Remove↺ Content-AwareHealClone Generative AI Brush Show Overlay Visualize Spots Click a spot, or drag over a line or wire. Atelier fills it from what's around it. 2 spotsDone Content-Aware / Heal / Clone: the mode for the next spot, and for the selected spot. Generative AI (Content-Aware only): ComfyUI fills new spots, and the selected one, instead of Atelier finding the fill in the photo (below). Size, Feather and Opacity set the brush and change the selected spot. Size is a share of the photo's long edge ([ ]); Feather is how softly the fill fades into the photo (⇧[ ⇧]). Double-click a name to reset it, or hold ⌥ and click Reset Brush to reset all three. Show Overlay shows every spot's outline. The selected spot always shows. Visualize Spots shows the photo in black and white to find dust (below). How many spots the photo has. The panel's reset button (↺), or Develop › Clear All Spots, deletes them all in one undoable step."},{"file":"remove.html","id":"generative","title":"Generative AI: people and large objects","page":"Remove: Content-Aware, Heal and Clone","text":"Content-Aware finds its fill in the photo, which is right for dust and wires but smudges a person or a large object in a busy scene. For those, choose Content-Aware, turn on Generative AI and paint over what should go. Atelier sends the area around the stroke to the Remove Object recipe in ComfyUI (the server, edit model and generation size are in Atelier › Settings… › AI), and the model paints it in. While ComfyUI works (a minute or more) the spot shows a Content-Aware fill, and the panel shows the progress with Cancel. You can keep making spots, or go to another photo; the fill is saved to its spot when it's done. The result is that spot's fill: it fades in through the spot's Feather and Opacity, and stays an edit. Your original is never changed, and no new photo is made. Press / for ComfyUI to make another. The current fill stays until the new one is in. Moving or resizing the spot makes a new fill once you let go, since the old one was made for where the spot was. Later White Balance and Exposure changes move the fill with the photo. After changing Lens Corrections or Transform, press / for a new fill. Needs ComfyUI Generative AI needs ComfyUI running with the Remove Object recipe, as for AI tools. If it can't be reached, the panel says so and the spot keeps its Content-Aware fill."},{"file":"remove.html","id":"visualize","title":"Visualize Spots","page":"Remove: Content-Aware, Heal and Clone","text":"Dust is hard to see on a sky or water at normal size. Turn on Visualize Spots (or press A while the tool is open): the photo turns white with its edges in black, so every speck shows as a dark ring. Drag Threshold to the right to show fainter specks. You can keep making spots while it's on; it turns off when you close the tool."},{"file":"remove.html","id":"keys","title":"Keys","page":"Remove: Content-Aware, Heal and Clone","text":"QOpen or close the Remove tool [ ] · ⇧[ ⇧]Brush size · feather /Next source for the selected spot (Content-Aware: another fill; Generative AI: ComfyUI makes another) AVisualize Spots Space-dragMove around the zoomed photo DeleteDelete the selected spot EscDeselect, then close the tool Copy and paste Paste Settings and Sync leave each photo's own spots alone, as Lightroom does by default: dust sits in a different place on every photo. Presets never carry spots. Large objects Content-Aware is made for dust, specks, blemishes, wires and small things on a plain or even background. A person or a large object in a busy scene can come out smudged; for those, turn on Generative AI. See also: Masking · AI tools · Develop basics"},{"file":"masking.html","id":"","title":"Masking","page":"Tools","text":"Edit just part of a photo: darken a sky, brighten a face, warm one corner. A mask says where; its sliders say what. Start a maskThe Masks panelLinear GradientRadial Gradient BrushLuminance RangeColor RangeDepth RangeSelect Subject and ObjectSelect SkySelect BackgroundSelect PeopleAdd, Subtract, IntersectMask sliders"},{"file":"masking.html","id":"start","title":"Start a mask","page":"Masking","text":"In Develop, click the Masking button (the dashed circle under the histogram) or press ⇧W. Or jump straight to a tool: M Linear Gradient, ⇧M Radial Gradient, K Brush, ⇧J Color Range. Click Create New Mask and pick a kind, then draw it on the photo. Move the mask's sliders (Exposure, Temp, Clarity…). They change only the masked area. Press Return or click Done. Masks keep working after Masking closes; open it again any time to change them. You can zoom and move around as usual while masking (Z, double-click, pinch, scroll, or hold Space and drag). Pins and handles stay the same size on screen."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"panel","title":"The Masks panel","page":"Masking","text":"›Masking↺ Mask 1👁 ▤Linear Gradient+ ◐Luminance Range∩ Mask 2👁 New Mask ⌄Add ⌄Subtract ⌄ Show Overlay Mask 1 … Temp, Tint, Contrast, Shadows, Whites, Blacks, Texture, Dehaze, Hue, Saturation, Sharpness, Noise, Moiré, Defringe Reset SlidersDone Masks, in the order you made them. Click one to select it; double-click to rename. The eye hides its effect. A selected mask lists its parts, each with a badge: + added, − subtracted, ∩ intersected. New Mask, and with a mask selected, Add or Subtract a part: Brush, Linear Gradient, Radial Gradient, Luminance Range, Color Range, Depth Range, Select Subject, Select Sky, Select Background, Select Object, Select People. Show Overlay (O) tints the selected mask red, so you can see exactly where it lands. The selected mask's sliders."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"linear","title":"Linear Gradient","page":"Masking","text":"A gradual change across the photo, like a graduated filter on a lens: darken a sky, lighten a foreground. With Show Overlay: red is where the change is full, fading to nothing at the bottom line. The result: the bright top darkened, the rest untouched. Draw: drag from where the change should be full to where it should fade out, as in Lightroom. To darken a sky, start at the top and let go at the horizon. Three lines show the start, middle and end. Lines close together give a hard edge; far apart, a soft one. Move: drag the pin in the middle. Widen or narrow one side: drag an outer line anywhere along it; it moves straight, keeping its angle. Turn: drag the dashed center line anywhere along it; the gradient turns around its pin. The pointer shows which one you're on. Move one end freely: drag the round handle on an outer line to move and turn that side at once."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"radial","title":"Radial Gradient","page":"Masking","text":"An ellipse: brighten a face, spotlight a subject, or (inverted) darken everything around it. Drag the pin to move it, the four square handles to resize, the round handle on top to rotate. The dashed inner ellipse shows where the full effect ends. A radial gradient lifts the man by the lake; the rest is slightly darkened with a second, inverted mask. Draw: drag from the center outward. Feather (0 to 100, default 50) softens the edge. The change is inside by default. Tick Invert to put it outside, for a custom vignette."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"brush","title":"Brush","page":"Masking","text":"Paint the mask where you want it. Drag on the photoPaint. Hold ⌥ while painting, or choose EraseErase. [ · ]Smaller · bigger brush. ⇧[ · ⇧]Less · more feather. Size, Feather and Flow are in the panel. Flow is how much each stroke adds, so low Flow builds up gradually. The pointer is a circle the size of the brush, with an inner circle where the feather begins."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"luminance","title":"Luminance Range","page":"Masking","text":"Selects by brightness: only the parts of the photo between two brightness levels (0 to 100). Smoothness softens the edges of the range. Overlay: the brightest areas Most useful combined. Intersect a Linear Gradient with a Luminance Range to darken only the bright sky, not the trees that reach into it."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"color","title":"Color Range","page":"Masking","text":"Selects by color: click a color on the photo, and everything of a similar color is selected, wherever it is. Choose Color Range from New Mask, Add or Subtract, or press ⇧J; the pointer becomes an eyedropper. Click the photoPick the color under the pointer. Drag over an areaPick the range of colors in the box, such as a whole sky from blue to pale. A wider spread of colors selects a wider range. ⇧-click or ⇧-dragAdd another color, up to five. ⌥-click a markerRemove that color. Click again (no key held)Start over with the new color. Overlay: the yellow kayak picked with one click Color Range 2 of 5 Invert The colors picked, up to five. Each also has a marker on the photo, filled with its color, where you clicked; right-click a swatch to remove it. Refine: left keeps only colors very close to the picks, right reaches further to similar colors. Lightness counts half as much as hue and saturation, so the same green in shade is still picked. The colors are read from the photo before your tone and color sliders, as Select Subject is, so changing Exposure or Saturation doesn't move the mask. While a Color Range part is selected, a drag on the photo picks colors rather than moving around; hold Space to move. Keep it to one place. Color Range selects that color everywhere. Intersect it with a Brush or a Radial Gradient (right-click the mask › Intersect Mask With) to change only one area's greens."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"depth","title":"Depth Range","page":"Masking","text":"Selects by distance: the parts of the photo between two depths, from 0 (the nearest part of the photo) to 100 (the farthest). Use it to cool a distant background, brighten a near foreground, or add haze only to faraway hills. Choose Depth Range from New Mask, Add or Subtract. The depth comes from a depth model, Depth Anything V2 Small (Apache 2.0 license). Download it once (50 MB) from the Depth Range settings or Atelier › Settings › AI › Masking Models; it then runs on your Mac in under a second per photo, and photos are never sent anywhere. Depth is relative: each photo's own nearest and farthest parts are 0 and 100. Click the photoPick the depth under the pointer: the range becomes that depth with a little either side. Drag over an areaPick the range of depths in the box, such as a whole hillside. ⇧-click or ⇧-dragWiden the range to take that depth in too. Depth Range Invert Near on the left, far on the right, with the chosen range outlined. From and To: the depths kept, 0 nearest to 100 farthest. A new Depth Range starts at 0 to 30, the nearest part of the photo. Smoothness softens the edges of the range, as in Luminance Range. The depth is found on the photo before your tone and color sliders, and its edges are snapped to the photo's own edges, so a mask doesn't spill past the outline of a tree or a person. While a Depth Range part is selected, a drag on the photo picks depths rather than moving around; hold Space to move. Combine it. Intersect a Depth Range with a Linea"},{"file":"masking.html","id":"subject","title":"Select Subject and Select Object","page":"Masking","text":"Select Subject finds the main subject (people, animals, things in front) with Apple's Vision, on your Mac. Select Object finds something inside a box you drag, so smaller subjects work too; drag the pin to move the box. Select Subject Both have the edge controls: Grow, Refine Edge and Feather (default 20). To change the background instead of the subject, use Select Background. If Vision finds nothing, the panel says so."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"sky","title":"Select Sky","page":"Masking","text":"Select Sky selects the sky in one step, to darken a bright sky, deepen its blue or warm a sunset without touching the land. It uses the same depth model as Depth Range (download it once, 50 MB): the depth model sees the sky as infinitely far, and the sky is the part at infinity that is open to the photo's top edge with no outline in between. Something that is only the farthest thing in the photo, like a stadium crowd behind a player, isn't at infinity, so it isn't taken, and neither is water reflecting the sky. A sunset that fades from pink to blue is followed, because the color changes a little at a time; a skyline, a tree line or a building against the sky stops it, because its outline is a sudden change, even when the city is as far away as the sky. Clouds the sky surrounds are taken, and so is sky seen through gaps, such as a tower's lattice or a tree's branches, where it matches the sky around it. The same rules hold for a blue noon, a sunset and a night sky. A far wall with no sky above it isn't taken, and a photo with no sky selects nothing and the panel says so. It has Grow, Refine Edge and Feather like the other edge controls. Refine Edge starts at 60, so the edge follows tree lines and rooftops; raise it for fine branches. Invert selects the land instead. Fog and haze. Where distant hills fade into a hazy sky, the depth model may see both as equally far and take the hills too. Subtract a Linear Gradient or a Brush from the mask there, or use Color Range on the sky i"},{"file":"masking.html","id":"background","title":"Select Background","page":"Masking","text":"Select Background selects everything except the main subject, in one step: the same subject Select Subject finds, turned inside out. Use it to darken, cool or soften what is behind a person or an animal. Select Background It has the same edge controls. Grow grows the background, so moving it right takes the background a little further onto the subject's edge, and left keeps it further away. Invert turns it back into the subject. If no subject stands out, there is no background to tell apart and the panel says so; try Color Range or a gradient instead."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"people","title":"Select People","page":"Masking","text":"Select People selects parts of people, like Lightroom's People masks. Apple's Vision finds each person, even one whose face is hidden by sunglasses or turned away, traces the face (jaw line, eyes, pupils, brows and lips) and outlines the person. A small model built into Atelier (Google's MediaPipe, about 8 MB) then tells each person's face skin, body skin, hair and clothes apart, so a beige sweater or a skin-colored scarf is clothes, not skin. Eyes, brows, lips and teeth come from the face's outline. Everything runs on your Mac; nothing is downloaded or sent anywhere. The line under the checkboxes says whether the built-in model ran; if it shows an orange warning, Hair, Clothes and Entire Person can't select anything and skin is guessed from color. Select People PersonAll People Entire PersonEntire Face Face SkinBody Skin EyebrowsEye Sclera Iris and PupilLips TeethHair Clothes Invert Person: everyone, or one person. People are numbered left to right by face. Tick any parts, in any combination. Face Skin is the face without the eyes, brows and lips; tick Lips too to include the mouth (they meet with no gap). Eyebrows, Eye Sclera (the whites of the eyes), Iris and Pupil, Lips and Teeth (only when the mouth is open) come from Vision's outline of the face, so a whitening or iris boost touches only that feature. Body Skin is the neck, arms and hands of the same person; Hair and Clothes (hats and scarves too) are their own parts, Entire Person is the whole person, and Entire Face i"},{"file":"masking.html","id":"edges","title":"Edge controls","page":"Masking","text":"Select Subject, Select Background, Select Object and Select People share Fine Edges and three sliders for the selection's edge, applied in this order: Fine EdgesRedraws the edge with BiRefNet (MIT license), which works out how much of each pixel is the subject. There are two models; download either or both: Fast (about 115 MB) takes a few seconds and gives a clean outline but misses loose strands, Hair Detail (about 470 MB) takes about half a minute and brings single strands through partly see-through. The first time, click Get and choose one; it turns on when the download is done. The menu beside the checkbox names the model that runs. Choose the other model there to switch, for every selection; if it isn't downloaded yet, it shows as Get Fast or Get Hair Detail with its size, and choosing it downloads it (with a progress bar and Cancel under the row) and then switches to it. Atelier › Settings… › AI › Masking Models does the same (Fine Edges uses), and its Remove deletes a model again. It runs on your Mac, on a square around the selection. Only the edge moves, within a band of where the selection was: strands outside it are added, background between strands inside it is taken out. With Select People only edges onto the background change, so Face Skin doesn't creep into Hair. Grow, Refine Edge and Feather shape the rest of the edge; they don't blur the strands Fine Edges finds. The line under the checkbox names the model and how long it took. The first Fast run after its dow"},{"file":"masking.html","id":"combine","title":"Add, Subtract and Intersect","page":"Masking","text":"A mask can have several parts: AddJoins a part to the mask: two radial gradients on two faces. SubtractCuts a part out: brush away the trees from a sky gradient. IntersectKeeps only where both overlap. Right-click the mask › Intersect Mask With. Right-click a mask for Rename, Duplicate, Invert, Intersect Mask With and Delete. Right-click a part for Invert, Switch to Add / Subtract and Delete. Delete removes the selected part (the whole mask if it was the last part)."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"adjust","title":"Mask sliders","page":"Masking","text":"Each mask has its own: Amount (0 to 200%, scales all of the mask's sliders at once), Temp, Tint, Exposure, Contrast, Highlights, Shadows, Whites, Blacks, Texture, Clarity, Dehaze, Hue, Saturation, Sharpness, Noise, Moiré and Defringe, then the mask's own Color Grading. Most work like their Basic and Detail twins. Double-click a name to reset it; Reset Sliders clears them all. Invert shows once, for what is selected: with a part selected it sits with that part's settings and inverts just the part; with the mask row selected it sits under Amount and inverts the whole mask. Temp and Tint go the Lightroom way: more Temp is warmer (yellow), more Tint adds magenta. Hue −180° to +180°Turns every color in the mask around the color wheel, keeping its brightness and strength. Right goes red → yellow → green → blue → magenta, left the other way; ±180° reaches the opposite color. The track shows where red goes. For fine moves, click the value and type it. Moiré 0 to 100Removes colored bands from fine patterns, such as fabric, screens or feathers, keeping their detail and brightness. Paint it only where the bands are, as in Lightroom. Defringe 0 to 100Removes purple and green fringes along edges inside the mask, with the same method as Lens Corrections › Defringe at its default colors. Use it where the whole-photo Defringe would touch colors you want to keep. Hue on a Color Range mask of the yellow kayak turns it orange without touching the red kayaks, the man or the lake."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"mask-grade","title":"Color Grading in a mask","page":"Masking","text":"Under the sliders, each mask has its own Color Grading, as in Lightroom Classic 16: the same wheels as the Color Grading panel, with the 3-Way view or one wheel at a time, Luminance, Blending and Balance. They work exactly like the panel's (drag the dot, Shift, Command and Option, double-click to reset, arrow keys over a wheel, right-click to copy a wheel and paste it, also between the panel and a mask), but only inside the mask, on top of the photo's own grade. The mask's Amount scales how strong the tints and Luminance are; the hues, Blending and Balance stay. Double-click the Color Grading heading to reset just the mask's grade; Reset Sliders clears it with the rest. The panel's Preset menu, color picker and eye buttons work on the whole photo, so they aren't in a mask. Only the sky under the gradient is graded: lavender shadows, a warm midtone and peach highlights turn the gray dawn into early sunrise. The man, the kayaks and the gravel keep the photo's own colors. Change your mind later. Masks stay on the same spot of the photo when you crop, straighten or change the Transform. They're saved with the photo, copied by Copy/Paste Settings and virtual copies, and can be included in presets. See also: History and undo · Create a preset"},{"file":"presets.html","id":"","title":"Presets","page":"Tools","text":"A preset is a saved set of slider values. Apply a look in one click, save your own, and use them on many photos at once. Preview and applyFrom the keyboardRecentAmountCreate a presetManage presets and groupsBuilt-in packsMany photos at once"},{"file":"presets.html","id":"apply","title":"Preview and apply","page":"Presets","text":"PRESETS＋⇕ Search Presets ⌄ 🕘 Recent2 Chrome 64🔒 Muted Chrome🔒 › User Presets3 › Atelier3 ⌄ Film9 Portrait 400🔒 Gold 200🔒 Vivid Slide🔒 › Black & White8 The Presets section in Develop's left column. ＋ creates a preset; ⇕ collapses every group. Search filters every group by name. Groups: yours first, then the built-in packs. Click a group to open or close it. Built-in presets show a lock. Rest the pointer on a preset to see it on your photo in the Navigator. Click to apply it. Recent: the presets you applied last, newest first. See Recent. Applying is one history step (Preset: Gold 200), so ⌘Z undoes it. A preset only changes the settings it includes; everything else on the photo stays as it was."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"keyboard","title":"Presets from the keyboard","page":"Presets","text":"Click a preset or a group (or press ⌃Tab until the left column has focus) and the list takes the arrow keys, like a list in Finder. The highlighted row shows a thin blue bar at its left edge. Tab moves between the list and the Search Presets field; Esc leaves the field. ↑ · ↓Previous or next row. On a preset, the Navigator previews it, just as resting the pointer does; the photo itself doesn't change. Return · SpaceApply the highlighted preset (one history step), or open or close the highlighted group. → · ←Open a group (again to go into it) · close it (from a preset, go up to its group). EscPut the preview away and give the arrows back to the photo."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"recent","title":"Recent","page":"Presets","text":"The Recent group at the top of the Presets section lists the last six presets you applied, from any group, newest first. Applying one again moves it back to the top. It's there as soon as you've applied a preset, and stays between launches. Right-click a preset in RecentRemove from Recent, then the preset's usual menu. Right-click the Recent groupClear Recent. Right-click photosApply Develop Preset › Recent has the same list. Recent hides while you search (the search already finds every preset). A deleted preset, or one in a hidden pack, drops out of the list."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"amount","title":"Amount","page":"Presets","text":"After you click a preset, an Amount slider appears at the top of the Presets section. Drag it from 0 to 200% to use the look more gently or more strongly. It updates the same history step, and goes away when you edit another setting or open another photo. Double-click it to go back to 100%."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"create","title":"Create a preset","page":"Presets","text":"Edit a photo until you like it. Click ＋ in the Presets header, or Develop › New Preset… (⇧⌘N). Type a Preset Name and pick a Group (or New Group…). Leave it as User Presets if you're not sure. Tick the settings the preset should carry. A setting still at its default shows “(no change)”. Click Create. What's unticked to start. Profile, White Balance, Lens Corrections, Transform, Crop and Masks depend on the photo, so they start unticked, as in Lightroom. The dialog remembers your last choice."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"manage","title":"Manage presets and groups","page":"Presets","text":"Right-click your presetApply · Update with Current Settings… · Rename… · Move to Group · Duplicate · Show in Finder · Delete… Right-click a built-in presetApply · Duplicate to User Presets (to start from it and change it) Right-click your groupCreate Preset in This Group… · Rename Group… · Delete Group… Right-click a built-in packHide Group. Develop › Manage Presets… shows or hides each pack. Photos that used a deleted preset keep their settings. Your presets are kept in one file shared by every catalog: ~/Library/Application Support/Atelier/Presets.json."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"built-in","title":"Built-in packs","page":"Presets","text":"See the built-in preset lookbook for every look, the subjects it suits and a before-and-after example rendered by Atelier. Built-in looks use Atelier’s editing controls and work on RAW, JPEG and HEIC. They leave exposure, white balance, profile, detail, lens corrections, transform, crop and masks to you. Film looks are interpretations of characteristic tones, colors and grain. Try the look in the Navigator first. If the effect is too strong, start around 50–70% with Amount."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"many","title":"Many photos at once","page":"Presets","text":"Right-click photos (grid, filmstrip, loupe or Develop) › Apply Develop Preset › a group › a preset. It applies to the whole selection at 100%. While importing: the Import dialog's Apply During Import picks a preset for every new photo. Copy and paste settings between photos: see Develop basics."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"panel-presets","title":"Presets for one panel","page":"Presets","text":"Tone Curve and Color Grading also have a Preset menu at the top of their panel, for looks that change only that panel: a contrast or matte curve, a teal-and-orange grade. Those presets are kept apart from this list so it stays a list of whole looks; they preview in the Navigator the same way. See Tone Curve presets and Color Grading presets. A Develop preset with only Tone Curve or Color Grading ticked still works too, and is how to save one together with other settings. See also: Match Look · Import photos"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"","title":"Built-in preset lookbook","page":"Tools","text":"Find a look by subject and see what it does to a reset photo at 100% Amount. Atelier rendered each pair using the same Develop controls you can edit in the app. Start with Presets to learn how to preview, apply and adjust the strength of a look. Film and print presets are creative interpretations. The original light, camera profile and existing edits influence the result. AtelierFilmBlack & WhitePortraitLandscapeStreetCinematicVintage & Matte"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"atelier","title":"Atelier","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":""},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-clean-product","title":"Clean Product","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Studio objects and white backgrounds. Brighter whites, open object shadows and restrained texture. Compare the white garlic skins and the shadows between the bulbs. Before Clean Product · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-fine-art-pigments","title":"Fine Art & Pigments","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Photographs of paintings and prints. A modest paper-white lift and clearer paper texture. Compare the painting's pigments and the gilded frame. Start with a neutral capture. Before Fine Art & Pigments · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-punchy","title":"Punchy","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Flat travel and everyday scenes. More midtone contrast and color separation, with legible shadows. Look at the faces of buildings and the brighter signs. Before Punchy · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"film","title":"Film","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":""},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-portrait-400","title":"Portrait 400","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Daylight portraits and weddings. Soft highlight roll-off, a small shadow lift and restrained color around skin. Fine, soft-edged grain. Before Portrait 400 · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-gold-200","title":"Gold 200","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Sunlit snapshots and travel. Amber warmth, rich red and yellow details and fine grain. Compare the warmer facades and the color of the sky. Before Gold 200 · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-pastel-400","title":"Pastel 400","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Open-shade and outdoor portraits. Brighter lower midtones, muted greens and blues, cooler shadows and a faint pink highlight tint. Fine, soft-edged grain. Before Pastel 400 · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-vivid-100","title":"Vivid 100","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Colorful travel and architecture. Saturated reds and clear blues with fine grain. Compare with Vivid Slide for more open shadows. Before Vivid 100 · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-vivid-slide","title":"Vivid Slide","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Sunlit landscapes and bold natural color. Deep blues and greens, firm shadows and crisp highlight separation. Look at the fairway and the clouds. Before Vivid Slide · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-chrome-64","title":"Chrome 64","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Daylight documentary and travel. Richer brick reds and deeper blues, with more subdued greens and overall saturation than Vivid Slide. Fine grain. Before Chrome 64 · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-tungsten-800t","title":"Tungsten 800T","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Mixed-light streets and bright practical lights. Cool shadows, visible fine-textured grain and red-orange halation around the brightest lights. Compare the lit bridge and the street lamps against the dark blocks. Before Tungsten 800T · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-expired","title":"Expired","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Creative faded snapshots. Fogged blacks, a green-to-pink color shift and uneven-looking coarse grain. Age and storage give real expired film a wide range of looks. Before Expired · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-instant","title":"Instant","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Casual portraits and snapshots. Creamy highlights, raised blacks, soft contrast and darker corners. Compare the faces and the frame edges. Before Instant · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"black-white","title":"Black & White","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":""},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-neutral","title":"Neutral","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for A plain starting point for monochrome. Even grayscale with Atelier's default B&W Mix. Check that there's no added curve, grain or toning when applied to a reset photo. Before Neutral · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-silver-gelatin","title":"Silver Gelatin","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Monochrome landscapes and gallery prints. Dense shadows, bright whites and a barely visible cool-shadow and warm-highlight split. Look for detail in the white clouds. Before Silver Gelatin · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-classic-400","title":"Classic 400","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Everyday monochrome reportage. Crisp detail, a mild yellow-filter effect and visible classic grain. Compare the sky and the building surfaces. Before Classic 400 · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-push-400","title":"Push 400","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Hard, grainy monochrome reportage. Coarse grain, strong contrast and harder shadows than Classic 400. Inspect grain at 100%. Before Push 400 · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-dark-sky","title":"Dark Sky","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Monochrome landscapes with blue skies. Blue sky and cyan tones darken while the grass and clouds stay bright. The effect depends on the original sky color. Before Dark Sky · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-infrared-glow","title":"Infrared Glow","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Sunlit foliage and landscapes. Greens and yellows become lighter, blues turn very dark, and bright edges bloom softly. The recipe works from visible colors. Before Infrared Glow · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-selenium","title":"Selenium","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Subtly toned monochrome prints. Dense blacks with a faint plum shift in the darker tones and near-neutral highlights. Compare with the warmer Sepia. Before Selenium · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-sepia","title":"Sepia","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Warm archival-style portraits. Chocolate-brown shadows, warm midtones and lighter cream highlights. Look for warmth across the grayscale image. Before Sepia · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"portrait","title":"Portrait","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":""},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-natural-skin","title":"Natural Skin","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Everyday portraits. Gently reduced reds and oranges, a small lift in skin mids, and slightly softer texture. Compare the cheeks and hands. Before Natural Skin · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-bright-airy","title":"Bright & Airy","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Bright, open-shade portraits and weddings. Lifted skin mids and open shadows, lighter surrounding colors, and recognizable dark tones. Look at the face and dark fabric. Before Bright & Airy · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-soft-glow","title":"Soft Glow","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Backlit portraits and soft window light. Gentle diffusion and softer texture with warm light spilling at bright edges. Compare the cheek highlights and the sea behind. Before Soft Glow · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-rich-skin","title":"Rich Skin","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Portraits with deeper skin tones. Open shadows and restrained golden warmth. Look for definition in the face without a strong red or yellow shift. Before Rich Skin · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-clean-studio","title":"Clean Studio","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Studio headshots and fashion. Crisper tonal separation with controlled skin saturation and clean light areas. Compare fabric and facial texture. Before Clean Studio · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-moody-portrait","title":"Moody Portrait","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Low-key editorial portraits. Deeper surroundings and subdued background color with warmer skin mids. The vignette concentrates attention toward the center. Before Moody Portrait · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"landscape","title":"Landscape","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":""},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-dramatic-sky","title":"Dramatic Sky","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Landscapes with bright textured clouds. A darker blue sky and more cloud texture, with less pressure on foreground shadows. Inspect the tree line for halos. Before Dramatic Sky · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-golden-hour","title":"Golden Hour","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Already warm side light and late-day landscapes. Amber sunlit grass and warmer trees, with cooler shadow separation. Compare the fairway and the clouds. Before Golden Hour · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-blue-hour","title":"Blue Hour","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Twilight and city scenes with warm lights. Cobalt-blue midtones and deeper shadows separate warm practical lights from the cooler surroundings. The strongest fit is an already cool scene. Before Blue Hour · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-deep-greens","title":"Deep Greens","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Forests and backlit leaves. Deeper greens, darker foliage shadows and warmer light. Compare the leaves to the wet path. Before Deep Greens · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-autumn-colors","title":"Autumn Colors","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Woodland with yellow and turning foliage. Yellow-green grass leans toward amber and existing oranges and reds become stronger. Compare the hillsides and the green slopes. Before Autumn Colors · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-haze-cut","title":"Haze Cut","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Distant hills and haze. Stronger distant contrast and clearer distant shapes. Check the headland in the fog and reduce Amount on already clear air. Before Haze Cut · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-wildlife-detail","title":"Wildlife & Detail","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Feathers and fur. More fine subject texture, brighter warm details and more subdued surroundings. Compare the breast feathers and beak. Before Wildlife & Detail · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"street","title":"Street","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":""},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-muted-chrome","title":"Muted Chrome","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Documentary street and subdued travel. Reduced blue and green saturation, firm shadows and restrained color that leaves reds readable. Compare the signs and the sky. Before Muted Chrome · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-hard-light","title":"Hard Light","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Direct sunlight and graphic street scenes. Deep shadow shapes and bold primary colors. Check separation in the street shadows before raising Amount. Before Hard Light · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-copper-negative","title":"Copper Negative","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Daylight street with warm brick and cool pavement. Contrasting copper reds and cyan-slate shadows, with visible classic grain. Compare warm signs to cool surfaces. Before Copper Negative · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"cinematic","title":"Cinematic","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":""},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-teal-orange","title":"Teal & Orange","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Cinematic portraits and mixed light. Teal-biased darks and warm orange mids, with less emphasis on greens. Check skin before increasing Amount. Before Teal & Orange · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-bleach-bypass","title":"Bleach Bypass","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Industrial, editorial and desaturated cinema. Hard darks, metallic highlights and color muted enough to approach monochrome. Compare the remaining colors in signs and vehicles. Before Bleach Bypass · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-noir","title":"Noir","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Low-key city and nighttime monochrome. Deep black-and-white contrast, bright highlights and darker corners. Look at the headlights and the shadowed buildings. Before Noir · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-night-city","title":"Night City","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Neon signs and urban night. Stronger cyan and magenta accents with deeper midnight shadows. Check that detail in the brightest lights remains legible. Before Night City · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-warm-drama","title":"Warm Drama","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Warm cinematic exteriors. Bronze shadows and ochre midtones with less blue and green saturation. Compare the stone facades and the blue taxi. Before Warm Drama · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"vintage-matte","title":"Vintage & Matte","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":""},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-faded-matte","title":"Faded Matte","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Muted editorial prints and street. Lifted charcoal blacks and softer highlights with some midtone contrast. Look at the deepest shadows for a restrained cast. Before Faded Matte · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-seventies","title":"Seventies","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Retro snapshots and warm street color. Mustard yellows, terracotta reds, softened blues and lightly faded blacks. Compare street signage and pale walls. Before Seventies · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-midcentury-postcard","title":"Midcentury Postcard","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Travel views with blue sky and warm details. Soft turquoise blues, coral warmth and gently lifted shadows. Compare the sky with the sunlit grass. Before Midcentury Postcard · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"look-cross-process","title":"Cross Process","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Best for Creative street and color experiments. Strong green-cyan shadows and yellow highlights from deliberately skewed color curves. Compare pale facades and the color of shadowed surfaces. Before Cross Process · 100%"},{"file":"preset-lookbook.html","id":"photo-credits","title":"Photos","page":"Built-in preset lookbook","text":"Every Before photo is Rosh’s own, taken from their RAW files, resized and stripped of metadata. The After images were edited with the named preset."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"","title":"Match Look to a Reference","page":"Tools","text":"Give your photo the color and tone of another picture: a photo you admire, a film still, one of your own edited shots, or a built-in color study. The result is ordinary sliders you can keep editing. Open itChoose a referenceBuilt-in ReferencesPreview and StrengthSkin TonesMatch AreasSeveral photos Reference Your photo Matched The golden light of a sunset over the bay carried over to a cool afternoon on the same water."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"open","title":"Open it","page":"Match Look","text":"In Develop: Photo › Match Look to Reference… (⇧⌘M). Or right-click photos anywhere › Match Look to Reference…, which opens them in Develop first."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"reference","title":"Choose a reference","page":"Match Look","text":"Choose File…Any image on your Mac: JPEG, PNG, HEIC, TIFF or a RAW. Or drag an image from Finder onto the reference box. Recent ReferencesThe last eight references. Only their location and a small thumbnail are kept; a moved file shows as Missing. Catalog Photo…Match one of your own shots, with its current edits. Good for making a set consistent."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"built-in","title":"Built-in References","page":"Match Look","text":"Click Built-in References below the reference preview. Each look has a portrait and a wider scene, such as an urban night, a winter landscape or desert action. They are flat vector color studies: every area is one plain color, so the look's palette shows clearly. Each portrait face has three flat planes, a highlight, a midtone and a shadow, and the backgrounds are grouped into a few key colors. The cards show each palette and list major color regions such as Face & Skin, Clothing, Sky and Ground. Use the labels to choose a study with a similar balance of colors. The chosen study appears on the left while Atelier fits your photo on the right. A study gives color only: while one is the reference, Tone is unticked and unavailable, so the match never changes your photo's Exposure or Tone Curve. A study's brightness comes from its drawing (a big dark coat, a pale sky), not from the look. Choose File… Catalog Photo… Built-in References The built-in gallery opens from the reference buttons. Wide studies use Whole Photo. Portrait studies start on Subject & Background: the study's background is matched to your photo's background, and that look covers your whole photo. Your hair and clothes take it too, as with a film grade; only skin is matched on its own (Skin Tones). The study's drawn clothes and hair don't set colors of their own, so no Match Look: Subject mask is added. Choose Whole Photo in Match Areas to match everything at once. Each portrait study has three skin samples, in th"},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"preview","title":"Preview, Strength and Show Differences","page":"Match Look","text":"The reference is on the left, a live preview of your photo on the right. Press and hold the preview to see it before the match. Choose what to match: White Balance (Temp and Tint), Tone (Exposure and the Tone Curve), Color (Saturation, HSL and Color Grading). Use Skin Tones to choose how to handle face color. Drag Strength (0 to 100) to blend between your current look and the full match. Click Show Differences to list every slider that will move, from its current value to the new one. Click Apply. Nothing changes on your photo until then; Cancel leaves it exactly as it was. Apply is one history step (Match Look: name). Everything it sets is an ordinary slider, so you can adjust, undo, copy or save it as a preset. No AI touches the pixels. The match is quick because Atelier renders your photo only a few times. It fits White Balance and Exposure on real renders, renders the photo once more, and works out every other slider from that render. It then checks the result with one more real render and settles Exposure on it. Match:White BalanceToneColor|Skin Tones:Protect Skin TonesShow Skin Match Areas:Subject & BackgroundBackground matches background; a Match Look: Subject mask matches the subject. Show DifferencesCancelApply Match: what the match may change. Turn off any of the three to leave those sliders alone. Skin Tones starts at Protect Skin Tones when Atelier finds a face in your photo and Off otherwise. Show Skin tints the skin it changes. Match Areas: Whole Photo, Subject "},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"what-moves","title":"What the match sets","page":"Match Look","text":"White Balance matches the color of the neutral parts of the scene (grays, whites, pale surfaces), not the average of everything, so a big green field or blue wall doesn't turn your photo magenta or orange. Tone sets Exposure (at most one stop either way), then a seven-point Tone Curve that follows the reference's contrast without lifting your shadows to gray. Blacks stay black and whites stay white unless the reference itself is faded or matte; then the curve lifts blacks (at most to 15%) or softens whites (at most to 92%) the same way. Color sets Saturation and HSL, then the Shadows, Midtones and Highlights wheels in Color Grading, so dark tones take the reference's shadow color and bright tones its light color, such as teal shade with warm light."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"skin","title":"Skin Tones","page":"Match Look","text":"Choose Off to let the ordinary match set face color. Your choice stays selected while the dialog is open, even when you switch references. Both treatments work on the face skin Select People finds (without eyes, brows and lips), in your photo and in the reference. Tick Show Skin next to the Skin Tones menu to see the skin tinted red on both pictures before you apply; on your photo it shows everything the skin masks change, the neck and hands included. If no face is found, Atelier falls back to the middle of each face box."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"protect-skin","title":"Protect Skin Tones","page":"Match Look","text":"A strong look, such as teal and orange or a cold film stock, can turn skin orange, green or grey. With Protect Skin Tones on, the skin Atelier finds is left out of the match, and the match may move its color by only a little (at most 5° of hue and 15% of saturation). The rest of the photo takes the full look; the skin is held at its own colors and brightness in its shadows, midtones and highlights with the Match Look: Skin masks. These masks use Select People's Entire Face: the whole face in one piece, lips, teeth, brows and eyes included, so nothing on the face keeps a look the skin was spared. You can edit them in Masking (⇧W) after Apply. Only when the masks can't hold the skin is the whole match made weaker. Atelier does this with ordinary sliders. It first turns back the HSL Hue and Saturation of the colors skin falls in (Orange, usually), when Color is on. If that isn't enough, it pulls White Balance, Saturation and Color Grading back toward where they were, just as far as needed. If that still leaves the skin outside the limits, Atelier backs off the rest of the match too. Show Differences lists the changes once the new match is ready. If the photo has no usable face skin, the dialog tells you."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"match-skin","title":"Match Skin Tone","page":"Match Look","text":"Choose Match Skin Tone to bring the color of faces toward the reference, tone by tone: skin in light, in midtone and in shade each takes the reference's color for that tone. Atelier uses three masks on everyone's Entire Face and Body Skin (Select People parts: the face with no holes at the teeth, brows or eyes, softened at its edge, and the neck, arms and hands so they follow the face; the whites of the eyes are left out; ellipses over the face boxes when Select People finds no one): Match Look: Skin gives the whole face one base color, and Match Look: Skin Shadows and Match Look: Skin Highlights add a soft Luminance Range on top so they reach mostly the darker or brighter skin and blend into the base without steps. The global look is fitted without the skin and kept from changing it much, so the skin masks only make a small, even change. With a built-in study, your own skin leads and the study only leans it: the hue moves part of the way toward the study's but stays a natural skin hue (an olive study gives a slightly yellower face, never a green one), the saturation goes up or down a little with how rich or pale the study's skin is but never to gray or orange, and the skin gets a touch of warmth, a bit more when the background is cool, so the face looks alive against it. Color is matched relative to brightness, so a darker or brighter face than the reference's gets the same look of saturation rather than the same numbers. The three masks are fitted together, so fixing the sh"},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"areas","title":"Match Areas","page":"Match Look","text":"When the reference shows different things from your photo, choose how they match in the Match Areas pop-up menu: Whole PhotoThe whole reference sets the look for the whole photo. This is the default. Subject & BackgroundAtelier finds the subject (people, animals, objects in front) in both pictures, so there is nothing to draw. The background is matched to the reference's background with the usual sliders. Then a mask named Match Look: Subject (a Select Subject mask) gets its own Temp, Tint, Exposure and Saturation, so your subject matches the reference's subject. You can edit or delete the mask in Masking like any other. Running Match Look again updates the same mask and keeps adjustments outside the checked categories. If Atelier can't find a subject in either picture, it matches the whole photo and says so. With a built-in study there is no subject mask: the background's look covers your subject too, and skin has its own masks. BoxesA box appears on each image: drag them over the parts that should match (skin to skin, sky to sky) and drag a corner to resize. The boxed areas count most, and the rest of each picture a little, so the whole photo still moves sensibly."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"several","title":"Several photos","page":"Match Look","text":"With several photos selected in the filmstrip, choose: Same settingsOne match, on the open photo, applied to all. Like Sync. Match each photoEach photo matched on its own, for mixed light. Runs in the background with progress and Cancel in the status bar. Match Skin Tone selects this option so the face masks are placed for each photo. See also: Masking · Presets · Color Grading"},{"file":"ai.html","id":"","title":"AI tools","page":"Tools","text":"Remove an object, replace a background or enlarge a photo with AI models running on your own Mac. The result is saved as a new photo stacked on the original, which never changes. Set up ComfyUIRemove ObjectReplace BackgroundUpscaleResultsLicenses"},{"file":"ai.html","id":"setup","title":"Set up ComfyUI","page":"AI tools","text":"Atelier sends photos to ComfyUI, a free app you run yourself; nothing is bundled with Atelier. Start ComfyUI (the desktop app, or from Terminal). In Atelier › Settings… › AI, set Server: the ComfyUI desktop app uses http://127.0.0.1:8000, ComfyUI from Terminal uses port 8188. Click Connect. Pick the Edit model and the Upscale model (4×) from what your server has, and a Generation size: 768 px (faster), 1024 px, or 1536 px (slower). The AI panel shows whether ComfyUI is reachable. If it says “ComfyUI not reachable”, start ComfyUI and click Check."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"open","title":"Open the AI panel","page":"AI tools","text":"In Develop, click the wand (the third icon under the histogram), or choose Photo › Remove Object…, Replace Background… or Upscale…. Pick a Recipe, set it up, then click Generate. Progress shows as “Generating… N s” with a bar and a percentage; Cancel stops it."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"remove","title":"Remove Object","page":"AI tools","text":"Choose Remove Object. Paint over what should go. Brush Size (or [ ]), Paint or Erase, and Clear to start again. Optionally say what it is (“a man”, “a sign”), which helps the model. Click Generate. Painted Only the area around your strokes is sent, at the generation size, so detail stays high; the result is blended back softly. (Illustration: the real result comes from the model.)"},{"file":"ai.html","id":"background","title":"Replace Background","page":"AI tools","text":"Atelier finds the subject (the background to replace is tinted on the photo), and you describe the new background in words. The subject's pixels are kept exactly."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"upscale","title":"Upscale","page":"AI tools","text":"Enlarge 2× or 4× with an upscale model, for big prints or heavy crops."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"results","title":"Results","page":"AI tools","text":"Each result is a 16-bit TIFF named name-AI Recipe.tif next to the original, stacked on it with a ✦ AI badge in the grid. Atelier records the recipe, models, licenses, prompt and seed with it, so any result can be traced and reproduced."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"licenses","title":"Licenses: what you can publish","page":"AI tools","text":"For your website or shop, use only results from permissively licensed models (Apache 2.0 or MIT), such as Qwen-Image-Edit-2511 (the default for Remove Object and Replace Background) or FLUX.2 [klein] 4B. Non-commercial models such as Qwen-Image-2.1, Fibo 2.0 or FLUX.2 [klein] 9B are for experiments only."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"workflows","title":"Your own workflows","page":"AI tools","text":"Save a ComfyUI workflow in API format as remove-object.json, replace-background.json or upscale.json in the folder from Settings › AI › Show Workflows Folder, and it replaces the built-in one. Title its nodes Atelier Image, Atelier Mask, Atelier Prompt, Atelier Sampler and Atelier Output, as in the example files there. See also: Edit In other apps · Stacks"},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"","title":"Lens Blur","page":"Tools","text":"Blurs the photo as a wide-open lens would, from how far away each part is: the background melts away, a foreground can go soft too, and bright points turn into bokeh. Use it to make a subject stand out from a busy scene, or to give a phone or small-sensor photo the look of a fast lens. Lens Blur has its own mode: set the blur there, then Save makes it a new photo stacked on the original (how). Open and saveApply and Blur AmountBokehFocal RangeRefine: Focus and BlurHair, step by stepVisualize DepthUp closeThe depth modelGood to know ›Lens Blur↺ ☑ Apply Bokeh Bokeh●○⬟◎⬮ Focal Range ⌖ Subject☐ Visualize Depth Refine Refine… Focus: 1 part BeforeCancel Save Only this mode shows the blur. Save makes it a new photo. Focal Range on the man (Near 70, Far 80) and Blur Amount 50: the kayaks and gravel in front go soft, and the far shore behind him a little, as they would through a fast lens."},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"mode","title":"Open, save and change it","page":"Lens Blur","text":"Lens Blur is a full-size, depth-aware blur, too heavy to redo on every slider move, so it has its own mode, as HDR does. Develop's panels never run it, and Exposure, Contrast and the rest stay as quick on a Lens Blur photo as on any other. Open a photo in Lens Blur: Photo › Lens Blur…, right-click › Edit in Lens Blur, or the aperture button in Develop's tool strip. Make your Develop edits first: the mode shows the photo as edited. The Lens Blur panel takes the place of Develop's panels, with every control on this page, and the photo shows a Lens Blur label. Before (\\) shows the photo without the blur. Lens Blur opens as a screen of its own: the photo and the Lens Blur panel only, without the Filmstrip, the left column, the filter bar, Develop's histogram and tool strip, or the Before/After views. When it closes, Develop comes back exactly as you left it. When something takes more than a moment, a small line beside Before says what is still working: rendering the blur, rendering it at full size, finding the depth, or Fine Edges finding the hair. Click Save (⌘S, or File › Save Lens Blur Photo). Atelier renders the photo at full size with its blur into a 16-bit ProPhoto TIFF next to the original, stacks it on the original (its thumbnail says Lens Blur), and opens it in Develop. Your Develop edits are baked in, so the TIFF starts with no edits of its own; edit it like any photo. Cancel (Esc) goes back to Develop and throws away everything you changed in the mode, as if you had ne"},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"apply","title":"Apply and Blur Amount","page":"Lens Blur","text":"Opening Lens Blur on a photo that never had it ticks Apply for you, so the photo blurs at once (Cancel takes that back). Otherwise tick Apply to turn Lens Blur on. The first time on a photo, Atelier focuses on its subject for you (see Focal Range). Blur Amount sets how strong the blur is for the parts farthest from the focus: 100 is about the blur of a portrait lens wide open. The blur is measured against the photo's size, so the preview and an export look the same."},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"bokeh","title":"Bokeh","page":"Lens Blur","text":"The shape that bright out-of-focus points take, as in Lightroom. Hover over a shape for its name. CircleRound and even, like a modern lens wide open. The default. BubbleRound with a bright rim, like a soap bubble. 5-BladeFive-sided, like a lens with a five-blade aperture stopped down a little. RingBright rings with a dark middle, like a mirror (catadioptric) lens. AnamorphicTall ovals, like an anamorphic cinema lens. Cat EyeCuts the shapes toward the photo's edges into cat's-eye shapes, as many older and fast lenses do. Higher gives the swirl a stronger pull. BoostBrightens the brightest highlights (those near white) before they are blurred, so they spread as bright bokeh instead of dull gray spots. It leaves the rest of the photo's light tones alone."},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"focal-range","title":"Focal Range","page":"Lens Blur","text":"The part of the photo kept sharp, as two depths from 0 (the nearest part of the photo) to 100 (the farthest). The strip above the sliders runs from near (white) to far (black) with the range outlined. Parts outside the range blur more the farther they are from it, in front of it as well as behind it, as a real lens does. ⌖ Focus pickerClick the button, then click the part of the photo to keep in focus. The range becomes the depths around that spot. The picker stays out, so another click moves the focus; press Esc or click the button again when done. SubjectKeeps the photo's subject in focus, as Select Subject finds it. This is also what Apply does the first time. Near, FarFine-tune the range by hand. A narrow range gives a thin plane of focus; a wide one keeps more of the scene sharp."},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"refine","title":"Refine: Focus and Blur","page":"Lens Blur","text":"The depth model is good but not perfect: a strand of hair, a hand held out, or a sign at the subject's side can land at the wrong depth. Refine fixes that, like Lightroom's Focus and Blur brushes, with the full set of selection tools and edge controls. Click Refine…. The refine tools open right there in the Lens Blur panel, with Done to close them, and the photo takes their pins and brush as in Masking. With Show Overlay on, the photo under the red shows without its blur, so the overlay comes up at once; the blur is back as soon as the overlay is off. Add to Focus marks what stays sharp whatever its depth; Add to Blur what blurs like the background around it. Pick how: Brush to paint (⌥ erases, [ and ] change the size), Select People (starts with Entire Person and Fine Edges, so hair is included), Select Subject, Select Object, a gradient, or a color or luminance range. Subtract cuts a part out. Select a part to refine its edge: Feather, Refine Edge, Grow/Shrink and Fine Edges, the same controls as in Masking. Select the area itself for its Amount (how fully it keeps sharp or blurs) and Invert. Show Overlay (O) tints it on the photo."},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"hair","title":"Hair and other fine edges","page":"Lens Blur","text":"Where a Focus area's edge is detailed, as Fine Edges draws hair, it works like a layer mask in Photoshop: the photo is blurred as if the area weren't there, and the sharp photo goes back on top through it. A strand that is half in the mask is half sharp hair over half blurred background. The depth model sees a head of hair as one solid shape, gaps included, so close to hair-like edges (2% of the photo's long edge) Lens Blur doesn't trust the depth: anything there that isn't in the area blurs at least as much as the background beyond it. The background between strands blurs with the rest instead of staying sharp. Along a single outline, such as a hat's or a shoulder's, the depth decides as usual, so a mask that sits a pixel inside the edge doesn't smear the subject's color outward as a halo. Where the edge is smooth, as with a feathered gradient, the area just lowers the blur, so focus falls off gradually. So the mask decides the edge. Select People with Fine Edges (Hair Detail) gives strand-by-strand detail; keep its Feather at 0 for hair, since Feather softens the edge outward onto the background. To touch it up by hand, dodge and burn the mask: Add to Focus › Brush with a low Flow (10 to 20) builds sharpness strand by strand as you go over it, and Subtract › Brush with a low Flow takes it back. A Blur area blurs like what is around it, so a patch the depth got wrong matches the background next to it. Where everything around it is sharp, it blurs as much as the farthest back"},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"hair-steps","title":"Step by step: a portrait with hair","page":"Lens Blur","text":"›Lens Blur · Refine 👁 Focus ● Select People Add to Focus ⌄Add to Blur ⌄Subtract ⌄ Show Overlay Select People Entire PersonEntire Face Fine EdgesHair Detail Brush Open the photo in Lens Blur (Photo › Lens Blur…), tick Apply and set Blur Amount. Tick Visualize Depth: the face and hair should be drawn lighter (in the Focal Range). If not, click the ⌖ picker, then an eye. Untick it. Click Refine…, then Add to Focus › Select People (1). Keep Entire Person (2) and Fine Edges (3) on, with the Hair Detail model if you have it (the menu at the right of the Fine Edges row switches models, and can get the one you don't have), and wait for the line that says it ran. Set the part's Feather to 0 (4), and Grow and Refine Edge to 0. Feather softens the edge outward onto the background, so the background right beside the hair would stay partly sharp. Zoom to 100% on the hair and tick Show Overlay (5, or O). Red is the Focus area: it should cover the strands, not the gaps between them. A strand without red will blur; a gap with red stays sharp. Dodge and burn the mask: with Focus selected, Add to Focus › Brush with a small Size, Feather 50 and Flow 15 (6). Each pass over a strand builds it up. Hold ⌥ (or use Subtract › Brush) at the same Flow to take red off a gap. If background between strands still looks sharp, paint it with Add to Blur › Brush (7): it blurs like the background around it. Untick Show Overlay to see the result. Zoomed in, the preview shows first and the full-size photo follow"},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"visualize","title":"Visualize Depth","page":"Lens Blur","text":"Shows the photo's depth instead of the photo: warm colors are near, cool colors far, and the parts inside the Focal Range are drawn lighter. Use it to see what the focus covers while you move Near and Far. It turns off when you close the Lens Blur mode. Visualize Depth on the photo above: the man and the beach at his distance are inside the Focal Range."},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"zoom","title":"Up close","page":"Lens Blur","text":"Lens Blur is meant to look like a lens, not a phone's portrait mode, even at 100%. Each pixel's blur grows smoothly with its distance from the focus: the ground just behind the subject is a little soft, farther away it melts, and the edge between subject and background stays as fine as in the photo. Atelier blurs at the full size of the photo where the blur is small and on smaller copies only where it is large enough that nobody could tell, so zooming in never shows a band or a halo where the blur changes. The full-size photo at 100% with Blur Amount 100: the man stays crisp, the red kayak beside him goes soft, and the lake behind him blurs gradually with distance."},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"depth-model","title":"The depth model","page":"Lens Blur","text":"Lens Blur uses the same depth model as Depth Range and Select Sky, Depth Anything V2 Small (Apache 2.0 license). If it isn't downloaded yet, the panel offers it (50 MB); it then runs on your Mac in under a second per photo, and photos are never sent anywhere. While the depth is being found the photo shows without the blur for a moment."},{"file":"lens-blur.html","id":"notes","title":"Good to know","page":"Lens Blur","text":"Lens Blur works on the whole, uncropped photo in linear light before the tone and color panels, so highlights spread as light does and your other edits apply to the blurred photo. Masks still select from the sharp photo. Edges of the subject stay as sharp as in the photo; something in front of the subject blurs over it, as through a lens. Where the depth model is unsure (fine hair, thin branches) the edge can blur a little: add a Focus area with Select People. In the Lens Blur mode, Before or the switch in the panel's header compares with and without the blur. New presets have a Lens Blur part, unticked by default, because the focus belongs to the photo it was set on. Copy and Paste Settings and Sync Settings carry it."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"","title":"Single-image HDR","page":"Tools","text":"Shape the tonal range and texture of one original in Atelier's HDR tool, save it as a 16-bit TIFF stacked with the original, then finish its color in Develop."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"start","title":"Open a photo in HDR","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"Select one photo and choose Photo › HDR… (⌃H), or right-click the photo › Edit in HDR. HDR opens in the same window; no view button is selected while it is open. Click Done at the foot of the right panel, or press Esc, to go back to the view you opened it from; or click Grid, Loupe or Develop (G, E, D). With unsaved changes you're asked first. HDR opens on a screen of its own, without the Filmstrip, with the same Before and After views and zoom as Develop. The left panel has the Presets, the Source and its HDR Result; the right panel has the Method, the Exposures (with Fusion) and the sliders. HDR works on the original's pixels, so a RAW keeps all its highlight and shadow range. Develop edits are not included; finish the color in Develop after saving. Nothing is written until you save, and the original is never changed. This version processes one image. It does not yet align or merge captured brackets or remove moving subjects. The darker and brighter exposures it makes from one photo show what the original already holds; they add no scene information."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"presets","title":"Presets","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"NaturalBalancedDramaticCrisp LinesLandscapePainterly Presets on a shared Help photo, one from each group. The outlined one is applied. The Presets section at the top of the left panel shows every preset as a small picture of your photo with that preset applied, in groups: Natural, Realistic, Dramatic, Architecture, Landscape and Fusion. Rest the pointer on a picture to preview it on the big photo, and click it to apply it. Applying is one undo step (⌘Z). The applied preset is outlined. A preset sets everything except Exposure, which stays with the photo; the pictures follow when you change it. Click the star on a picture, or right-click it › Add to Favorites, to collect it in Favorites at the top. Click + in the Presets header to save the current look as your own preset, in User Presets. Right-click one of yours to Rename… or Delete… it. Photos already saved with a preset keep their look. Click a group's name to open or close it."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"controls","title":"Tone and texture","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"Tone Mapping MethodFusion ⌄ Tone Texture Color One Tone Mapping panel: the Method (Spacing shows for Fusion), range compression, then the tone of the bright and dark areas, texture and color. MethodNatural (the default) compresses the lighting region by region and restores midtone contrast: crisp and close to the original. Fusion blends three exposures of the photo, darker, as shot and brighter (see Exposures), taking each region from the exposures where it is best exposed: more open shadows and softer highlights, like an exposure-fusion HDR. Deep blacks stay black. Double-click the name to go back to Natural. SpacingFusion only: how many stops under and over the photo its darker and brighter exposures are, from ±1 to ±3 EV (default ±2). Wider spacing opens the shadows and holds back the highlights more. ExposureGlobal brightness in stops, from −5 to +5. Start here if the original looks dark. RangeCompresses broad lighting differences from 0 to 1, opening dark structure and holding back bright areas while retaining local detail. Higher values create a stronger HDR effect. With Fusion, Range goes from the single exposure (0) to the full blend (1). HighlightsDarkens (left) or brightens (right) the bright areas after tone mapping, from −1 to +1, as Develop's Highlights does. Move it left to bring back sky and window detail. ShadowsBrightens (right) or darkens (left) the dark areas, from −1 to +1, as Develop's Shadows does. Each works on whole areas, so texture inside them moves "},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"exposures","title":"Exposures","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"−2.0 EV 0 EV +2.0 EV The Exposures strip under Method and Spacing: the photo darker, as shot and brighter, by Fusion's Spacing. With the Method set to Fusion, Tone Mapping shows Exposures under Method and Spacing: the photo at the three exposures Fusion blends, darker and brighter by the Spacing, around the Exposure slider. The darker one shows what the brightest areas hold; the brighter one shows what is in the shadows. Click the darker or brighter picture to leave it out of the blend (it dims and shows a crossed eye); click again to put it back. Leaving out the brighter one keeps shadows deeper; leaving out the darker one keeps highlights brighter. The middle one is always used. Each click is one undo step. They are made from the one original, so they don't add detail it doesn't have; a RAW holds the most. Merging real bracketed photos is planned. Natural and Fusion at the same Range on a shared Help photo. Fusion opens the dark lagoon and trees and keeps the sunrise sky soft; Natural keeps more contrast."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"compare","title":"Compare and zoom","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"Below the photo, choose Before and After: Off, Side by Side or Slider, as in Develop (Y cycles them). Drag the slider's divider to move it. \\ shows the original. Zoom works as everywhere in Atelier: double-click, Z, pinch, the zoom buttons, or ⌘= and ⌘−; drag or scroll to move around. The zoom and the spot in view carry over to and from Loupe and Develop. At 100% and closer the full-size original is used, so judge Detail there."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"save","title":"Save, reopen and new versions","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"Click Save HDR at the foot of the right panel, or choose File › Save HDR (⌘S). Atelier renders a full-size, profiled 16-bit sRGB TIFF next to the original (or in Pictures/Atelier/Edits if that folder is read-only), with an HDR project (.atelierhdr) beside it. The TIFF appears stacked with the original and becomes the selected photo. Saving again updates the same TIFF; the button reads Save once there is one. In HDR, ⌘S is Save HDR; in the other views it stays Library › Write XMP for Selection. To change the HDR later, select the HDR TIFF and choose Photo › HDR… again: its project opens with the sliders where you saved them. Opening the original in HDR again starts a new, separate HDR. File › Save HDR As New… (⇧⌘S), also in the left panel, makes a separate project and TIFF and keeps the earlier one. If you move to another photo, switch to another view or quit with unsaved HDR changes, Atelier asks whether to Save, Cancel or Don't Save. Don't Save keeps the last saved TIFF as it was, or makes nothing if you never saved."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"recovery","title":"Cancellation and recovery","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"While saving, the bar under the photo shows progress and Cancel (or press Esc); the last saved TIFF and project are kept. Each project keeps its own verified copy of the original and the current and previous saved results. Reopening checks them. If the project's copy of the original is missing or changed, HDR says so; click Relink Source… and choose an identical copy of the original. Its contents are checked first, so a different photo is rejected. A save interrupted while the TIFF was being written is put back from the project when it is reopened. Keep the project and its TIFF when backing up; the catalog backup alone does not contain them. Before November 2026 HDR was a separate app. HDR TIFFs it saved while Atelier was closed are added the next time Atelier opens, and its projects open in HDR like any other. See also: Develop basics · Stacks · Edit In other apps"},{"file":"export.html","id":"","title":"Export","page":"Share","text":"Save finished photos as JPEG, HEIC, TIFF or PNG, with your edits, at the size and color space you need, with your watermark if you want one. Save the choices you use often as presets. Originals are never changed."},{"file":"export.html","id":"open","title":"Open the Export dialog","page":"Export","text":"Select photos and choose File › Export… (⇧⌘E), click Export in the toolbar, or right-click › Export…. In Loupe and Develop it exports the photo you're on (or the filmstrip selection). Export 12 Photos Atelier Website Full-Size JPEG Full-Size TIFF User Presets Shop + Watermark + − FormatJPEG Quality Resize to fit2048pixels Color spacesRGB (web and social) SharpeningFor screen MetadataAll except location Add watermark Save to~/Pictures/Atelier ExportsChange… Show in Finder when done CancelExport 12 Photos Presets fill in everything on the right in one click (see Export presets). The preset that matches the current settings is highlighted; change any setting and the highlight goes, as in Lightroom. Format: JPEG and HEIC (with Quality, 50 to 100), TIFF (16-bit) or PNG. Resize to fit: the long edge in pixels. Off keeps the full size. Color space: sRGB for web and social, Display P3 or Adobe RGB (1998). Sharpening: None or For screen. Metadata: All metadata, All except location (removes GPS), or None. Add watermark puts your name or logo on every photo (see Watermark). Save to a folder. Atelier remembers your last choices."},{"file":"export.html","id":"presets","title":"Export presets","page":"Export","text":"Atelier has three built-in presets: Website (JPEG 85, 2048 px long edge, sRGB, sharpened for screen, no location), Full-Size JPEG (JPEG 95, all metadata) and Full-Size TIFF (16-bit, Adobe RGB, for print or further editing). Hover over a preset to see what it sets. To save your own, set everything up and click + under the list, then name it. A preset keeps the format, quality, size, color space, sharpening, metadata and watermark. It doesn't keep the folder. Right-click a user preset for Update with Current Settings or Delete…. To delete the highlighted preset you can also click −. Built-in presets can't be changed. Using the name of a preset you already have replaces that preset. User presets are shared by every catalog."},{"file":"export.html","id":"watermark","title":"Watermark","page":"Export","text":"Turn on Add watermark to see its settings, with a preview of the first photo above them showing exactly where the watermark will go. StyleText (it starts as © and the year with your name; type anything) in White or Black, or Image: click Choose… and pick a logo. A PNG with a transparent background works best. SizeThe watermark's width as a share of the photo's width. Because it's a share, the watermark looks the same on a 2048 px website JPEG and a full-size TIFF. Opacity100% is solid; lower lets the photo show through. PositionClick one of the nine dots: a corner, an edge or the center. InsetThe gap between the watermark and the photo's edges, as a share of the photo's short side. ShadowA soft drop shadow that keeps white text readable on bright skies. Double-click a slider's name to reset it. The watermark is drawn after resizing and sharpening, so it stays crisp and is never sharpened. If the text is empty or no image is chosen, Export stays dimmed until you fix it or turn the watermark off."},{"file":"export.html","id":"while","title":"While it runs","page":"Export","text":"Exports run in the background, with progress and Cancel in the status bar. Keep working meanwhile. Files keep their original names. An existing file is never replaced: a number is added instead. A virtual copy is named name-Copy1.jpg. Keywords, title, caption and rating are written into the files (unless Metadata is None). Photos whose drive is offline are listed under Show Problems; the rest still export. A photo on a NAS that's offline still exports from its copy on this Mac, if there is one, and Show Problems notes that it couldn't be checked. Photos on a NAS are read through the cache of originals, once each. One whose file changed on the NAS since import isn't exported; a sheet at the end lets you choose for it (see When the original changed). See also: Edit In other apps · Settings › Export"},{"file":"edit-in.html","id":"","title":"Edit In other apps","page":"Share","text":"Send a photo to Photoshop, Affinity Photo, Pixelmator Pro or any other editor, and get the result back in Atelier, stacked on the original."},{"file":"edit-in.html","id":"how","title":"How it works","page":"Edit In other apps","text":"Select a photo and choose Photo › Edit In › Edit in Photoshop (the first app in the list is ⌥⌘E), or right-click › Edit In. Atelier saves a copy with your edits as name-Edit.tif next to the original (or in Pictures/Atelier/Edits if that folder is read-only) and opens it in the app. The copy appears in the grid right after the original, with a ◪ Edit badge. It joins the same imports and collections and gets the rating, label, title, caption and keywords. Save in the other app. Atelier sees the save and updates the preview. Edit In on a photo that already is an edit opens that file as it is, so you can keep working on it. For a photo on a NAS whose file changed there since import, Atelier sends the others and then asks what to use for it (see When the original changed). HDR isn't an Edit In app: it is a view of Atelier. Choose Photo › HDR… (⌃H). See Single-image HDR."},{"file":"edit-in.html","id":"apps","title":"Choose the apps","page":"Edit In other apps","text":"The menu lists the editors Atelier finds (Photoshop, Affinity Photo, Pixelmator Pro, Photomator, GIMP, Krita). Other Application… adds any app. In Atelier › Settings… › Edit In you can add, rename and remove apps, Find Installed Editors, and choose for each: File16-bit TIFF (best quality) or JPEG. ColorProPhoto RGB (the default, keeps every color), Adobe RGB, Display P3 or sRGB. Include develop adjustmentsOn: the app gets your edited photo. Off: it gets the photo without Atelier's edits. See also: Stacks · AI tools · Export"},{"file":"settings.html","id":"","title":"Settings","page":"Set up","text":"Open Atelier › Settings… (⌘,). Changes take effect at once. Pick a pane in the sidebar on the left; its settings show on the right. The sidebar groups the panes: Atelier (General, Appearance, Keyboard), Photos (Import, Storage, Develop, Export, Metadata) and Editors & AI (Edit In, AI). The sidebar has the keyboard when Settings opens: ↑ ↓ move between panes. After you click into a pane, click the sidebar again to get the arrows back (the chosen row turns from gray to blue). Settings reopens on the pane you used last. Click a section's header band to close or open it, as with the panels in the main window; Atelier remembers which are closed. GeneralAppearanceKeyboardImportStorageDevelopExportMetadataEdit InAIAgents"},{"file":"settings.html","id":"general","title":"General","page":"Settings","text":"CatalogWhere the catalog is (Show in Finder) and how many photos it holds. See If the catalog cannot open. BackupsThe catalog is backed up when Atelier quits; choose how many to keep, or Back Up Now. Splash ScreenShow it at launch, Show Splash Folder (add your own images, ideally 1600 × 1000 px or larger; each launch shows the next), and Preview Splash Screen. Private PhotosHow long Atelier can go unused before private photos hide again: 5 to 60 minutes (10 by default) or Never. They always hide when the Mac sleeps or locks, and when Atelier quits. FeedbackPlay a sound and a puff of smoke when deleting. PreviewsHow much space thumbnails and previews use."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"appearance","title":"Appearance","page":"Settings","text":"Pick a theme from small previews. It changes every window at once. SystemFollows macOS, light or dark. Light · DarkmacOS's own styles. GraphiteCharcoal panels and a dark surround, like Lightroom. DarkroomNear black everywhere, so only the photo stands out. Neutral GrayA mid-gray surround, for judging color and brightness."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"keyboard","title":"Keyboard","page":"Settings","text":"The Keyboard Map at the top shows each key with a small picture of its command: stars for ratings, label colors, flags and tool symbols. Choose ⇧ Shift, ⌃ Control or ⌥ Option to see the keys used with them. Hover a key to see its command; click it to jump to that command in the list. Search commands or keys at the bottom finds a command by name, group or key. Every keyboard command (ratings, flags, labels, views, tools, Masking, Guided Upright, and ⌃Tab between areas) with its key. Click a key and press a new one, with ⌃, ⌥ or ⇧ if you like; a key moves from any command that had it. Arrows, Tab, Return, Esc, Delete, F6 (Show Filmstrip) and ⌘ shortcuts stay fixed. Delete removes a key, Esc cancels, Restore Defaults goes back to Lightroom's keys. Help › Keyboard Shortcuts (⌘/) lists them all; its Change Keys… button opens this pane."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"import","title":"Import","page":"Settings","text":"The default for new imports (leave files where they are, or copy into the library folder), the library folder, and Show RAW+JPEG pairs as one photo."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"storage","title":"Storage","page":"Settings","text":"The NAS shares, drives and folders you keep photos on: add one with +, see each one's status, photos and free space, connect or disconnect a share, and remove one. Cache of Originals sets how much of this Mac's disk keeps copies of photos on network shares (5 GB to 200 GB, 20 GB to start), shows how much is in use (photos kept with Keep on This Mac are counted apart), and clears it; tick Also remove kept photos in the alert to clear those too. Backups chooses where else the catalog is backed up (a drive, NAS, cloud drive folder or S3 bucket), when, and how many are kept, with Back Up Now. Smart Previews shows how many photos have one and the space they take, with Discard All…; each location's details have Make smart previews for photos here. See Storage locations."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"develop","title":"Develop","page":"Settings","text":"Double-click or Z zooms to50%, 100% (1:1), 200% or 400%. When you move to another photoOpen it at Fit or Fill (the default); return to its last zoom; or keep the same zoom, as in Lightroom. PanelsSolo mode (opening a panel closes the others) and the histogram's clipping warnings."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"export","title":"Export","page":"Settings","text":"The default export folder, and whether to show exported files in Finder. Format, size, color, watermark and presets are in the Export dialog."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"metadata","title":"Metadata","page":"Settings","text":"Automatically write changes into XMP sidecars, including current Develop edits, masks, retained history and snapshots for Atelier recovery. Other apps see the standard metadata, such as ratings, keywords and captions. See XMP sidecars."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"edit-in","title":"Edit In","page":"Settings","text":"Each external editor has its own section: the app, its name in the menu, the file it is sent (TIFF or JPEG), the color space and whether develop adjustments are included. The − in a section's header removes that app; Add App… and Find Installed Editors are at the bottom. See Edit In."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"ai","title":"AI","page":"Settings","text":"The ComfyUI server and Connect, the edit and upscale models, generation size, and the workflows folder. See AI tools. Masking Models shows the people model built into Atelier and lets you download or remove the two Fine Edges models (Fast, about 115 MB; Hair Detail, about 470 MB) and choose which one runs (one run takes about 18 GB of memory for Fast and 40 GB for Hair Detail; on a Mac with less, Settings says so under the model, and if memory runs out the run stops with a message instead of slowing Atelier), and the depth model for Depth Range (about 50 MB)."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"agents","title":"Agents","page":"Settings","text":"Chat apps (OpenCode, Cherry Studio, Ollama with ollmcp, LM Studio, Claude, Codex) can work with your library through Atelier's agent connection (MCP). With Look they search photos, read details, Develop settings and cull groups, and list the menu commands. With Organize they can also select photos in Atelier, rate, flag, label, add or remove keywords, set titles and captions, add photos to collections, make virtual copies, and run library commands. With Edit they can also edit in Develop: set or nudge sliders (Exposure, Contrast, White Balance, HSL, Color Grading, Detail, Effects…), run Auto and Auto White Balance, apply a Develop preset, sync settings from one photo to others, reset settings, crop, straighten, rotate and flip, add masks (Select Subject, Select Background, Select Object, Select People, Linear and Radial Gradients, Luminance Range; Brush and Color Range stay yours), match a look to a photo or a built-in study, and run Develop commands. Exports and other apps come in a later version, and no app can delete anything."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"agent-develop","title":"When an app edits in Develop","page":"Settings","text":"Each edit is one step in the photo's History, named for the app, such as Exposure +0.50 (Claude), so you can step back past it as with your own edits. The notice at the bottom of the photo offers Undo, which adds an Undo … step that puts the settings back, but only while nothing has changed them since. If you're dragging a slider or handle when the app asks, Atelier finishes your drag first. Changing a slider in a panel that's switched off turns the panel on, as when you do it. A value past a slider's end stays at the end, and White Balance Temp can't be nudged on a photo that's As Shot (the app can set it instead). Develop menu commands an app runs, such as Develop › Rotate Left, act on the photo open in Develop. Auto and Match Look look at each photo's pixels first. While they do, the status bar at the bottom of the window shows the app and how far it has got, such as Claude: Auto Tone, 3 of 12, with Cancel; cancelling changes nothing. A photo you edit while it measures is left as you made it. A mask an app adds shows in the Masks panel like yours, and a crop in the crop tool, so you can adjust either by hand."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"agent-changes","title":"When an app changes photos","page":"Settings","text":"Each change shows a notice at the bottom of the photo, such as Claude: Rate 3 stars (37 photos), with Undo for about ten seconds. The app can undo its own change too. Undo works only while the change is still the latest on those photos: if you, or another app, changed them since, Atelier keeps that later work and says why it can't undo. Undo puts back the rating, flag, label, keywords, title, caption and collections; a new collection stays (empty), and virtual copies are removed with Photo › Remove Virtual Copy…. A change to more photos than Ask before one change touches more than allows asks you first, in Atelier's window."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"agent-batch","title":"Several changes at once","page":"Settings","text":"An app can send several changes in one go, such as “label the picks green, rate them 4 stars and add them to Portfolio”. Normally each change is its own, with its own notice and Undo, and the app stops at the first one that fails. An app can also ask for all or nothing: then the whole batch is one change, with one notice and one Undo, and its Develop edits are one step in each photo's History, named for the batch. If any part fails, Atelier puts back what the batch already did and changes nothing. All-or-nothing batches can rate, flag, label, add keywords, add to collections and edit in Develop with the app's own tools; Develop menu commands and virtual copies have to be sent one by one. When a batch touches more photos than Ask before one change touches more than allows, Atelier asks once, for the whole batch."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"agent-photos","title":"Photos in the chat","page":"Settings","text":"A chat app that shows app views, such as Docent, shows the photos an app finds as a strip of cards in the conversation, with each photo's stars, flag and color label. Move with the ‹ and › buttons or the arrow keys, and click N more at the end to load the next photos. Select a card and click Show in Atelier (or double-click it, or press Return) to select it in Atelier's window; that needs Organize or more. Ask about this photo sends a message about it to the chat for you. The pictures appear only while Let agents see photo previews is on; otherwise each card says No preview. Private photos never appear. Chat apps without app views, such as Cherry Studio and OpenCode, show the same results as text."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"agent-docking","title":"Docking beside a chat app","page":"Settings","text":"A chat app built for it, such as Docent, can line Atelier's window up beside its own: Docent stays on the left and Atelier fills the right, the same height. Only the chat app's own window code can do this; no model can move Atelier's window. While docked, Atelier keeps the photo and collapses side panels when there isn't room: the left panel goes first, then the right one, and they come back as soon as the window is wide enough again. Drag Atelier's window away by its title bar, or resize it, to undock it (the chat app is told). Undock in the Access section does the same. When the chat app undocks Atelier itself, it either leaves the window where it is or puts back Atelier's own size and place. Atelier's window docks at 520 points wide or more, and not in full screen. AccessAllow chat apps to use Atelier (on by default; turning it off disconnects every app). Let agents see photo previews (off): when on, an app can look at a photo as a JPEG up to 1024 px. Include location in photo details (off). Ask before one change touches more than 10, 25, 50 (the default), 100, 250 or 500 photos, or Never ask. Private photos are never shared, whatever View › Show Private Photos says. While a chat app has docked Atelier's window, a line here says so, with Undock. AppsEvery app that has connected, with a green dot while it is connected and the menu of what it may do: Don't Allow, Look, Organize, Edit or Everything. The first time an app connects, Atelier asks in its own window and you pick o"},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"","title":"Connect a chat app","page":"Set up","text":"Ask a chat app about your library: “how many photos did I pick from the last import?”, “which photos from Iceland have five stars?”, “what Develop settings does this one use?”. Atelier speaks MCP, the standard way chat apps reach other apps, so any chat app that supports MCP servers can connect, with a free, local or hosted model. What chat apps can do. With Look, chat apps read: search photos, look at their details, Develop settings and cull groups, and list the menu commands. With Organize they can also select photos in Atelier, rate, flag, label, add keywords, add notes and add to collections, each with Undo (see When an app changes photos). With Edit they can also edit in Develop: set or nudge sliders, run Auto, apply presets, sync and reset settings, crop and straighten, add masks and match a look, each a step in the photo's History with Undo (see When an app edits in Develop). They can also send several changes in one go, all or nothing with one Undo (see Several changes at once). In chat apps that show app views, such as Docent, found photos appear as a strip of cards (see Photos in the chat). Exporting from a chat comes in a later version; every menu command is already built so it can reach chat apps without extra work. How it worksOpenCodeCherry StudioOllama (ollmcp)LM StudioOpen WebUIClaude DesktopClaude CodeCodexFrom your iPhoneWhich modelPrivacyIf it doesn't work"},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"how","title":"How it works","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"Every chat app below starts the same small helper, which is inside Atelier, with no arguments: /Applications/Atelier.app/Contents/MacOS/atelier-mcp Atelier must be open. The helper passes messages to the Atelier window; it never opens the catalog itself. Atelier asks first. The first time an app connects, Atelier asks in its own window whether to allow it, and what it may do: Don't Allow, Look, Organize, Edit or Everything. Today every level can only read (see the note above); the higher ones are for organizing, editing and, with a second question in Atelier, exporting, as those arrive. Change it later in Atelier › Settings… › Agents. Undo will work. When chat apps can make changes, each one will be one step in the photo's history, with a plain name, that ⌘Z undoes like your own edits. Nothing is deleted. Chat apps can't remove photos or move files to the Trash; they tell you to use Photo › Remove Photo… yourself."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"settings-agents","title":"Settings › Agents","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"Atelier › Settings… › Agents is where you manage this (details in Settings › Agents): AccessAllow chat apps to use Atelier (on; turning it off disconnects every app), Let agents see photo previews (off) and Include location in photo details (off). AppsEvery app that has connected, with a green dot while it's connected and a menu of what it may do. Changing it reconnects the app; − forgets the app, so Atelier asks again. Apps started from Terminal are listed as Terminal. SkillsWorkflows you write for chat apps, with Atelier's commands in them. See Skills. Connect an AppThe helper's path with Copy. Choose an app in Setup for and click Copy Setup; the line under it says where to paste. ActivityWhat each app did, newest first. Select lines and click the copy button, or right-click for Copy and Copy All, to paste into a bug report. Running Atelier from Xcode instead of /Applications? Build the helper once: Product › Scheme › atelier-mcp, then Product › Build (⌘B). Then use the path and setup that Connect an App shows."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"opencode","title":"OpenCode (free models)","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"OpenCode is a free chat app for Terminal with free hosted models, and it can use your Ollama models too. Add Atelier to ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json. The easy way: Settings › Agents › Connect an App, choose OpenCode in Setup for, click Copy Setup, paste it into Terminal and press Return. It runs a short script that adds Atelier and keeps what's already in the file. Or add it yourself, directly under \"mcp\": { \"$schema\": \"https://opencode.ai/config.json\", \"mcp\": { \"atelier\": { \"type\": \"local\", \"command\": [\"/Applications/Atelier.app/Contents/MacOS/atelier-mcp\"], \"enabled\": true } } } Check it with opencode mcp list. \"enabled\": false turns it off without deleting it. (opencode mcp add didn't save anything when we tried it, so use the file.) Pick a model: type /connect to sign in to the OpenCode provider, then /models and choose one with no price (free models are free for a limited time and change often), or one of your Ollama models. Ask: “Use Atelier to list my collections.”"},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"cherry","title":"Cherry Studio","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"A desktop chat app that works with Ollama models and hosted ones. Open Cherry Studio's settings and choose MCP Server, then Add server. Name Atelier, Type STDIO, Command /Applications/Atelier.app/Contents/MacOS/atelier-mcp, Arguments left empty. Click Save. In a chat, turn on MCP in the chat box and select Atelier. Pick a model that can call tools, such as Gemma 4 or Qwen 3.6 from Ollama."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"ollama","title":"Ollama, with ollmcp (Terminal)","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"Ollama runs the model but doesn't connect to MCP servers itself, so a chat app sits in between. Any app on this page can use Ollama models; ollmcp (mcp-client-for-ollama) is the simplest one in Terminal. It needs Python 3.11 or later, with Ollama running. uv tool install --upgrade ollmcp # or: pip install --upgrade ollmcp ollmcp mcp add atelier --scope user -- /Applications/Atelier.app/Contents/MacOS/atelier-mcp ollmcp mcp list ollmcp # then pick qwen3.6 or gemma4 and chat --scope user saves it in ~/.config/ollmcp/mcp.json for every folder. Without it, it's saved only for the current folder."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"lmstudio","title":"LM Studio","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"In the right sidebar, open the Program tab and click Install › Edit mcp.json (LM Studio 0.3.17 or later). Inside \"mcpServers\": { … }, add: \"atelier\": { \"command\": \"/Applications/Atelier.app/Contents/MacOS/atelier-mcp\", \"args\": [] } Save, load a model that supports tool use, and turn on the Atelier tools in the chat."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"openwebui","title":"Open WebUI","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"Open WebUI connects to MCP servers over the network (Streamable HTTP, v0.6.31 and later), not to a helper it starts itself. Atelier's own network listener takes only paired phones (see From your iPhone), so put the helper behind mcpo, which serves it to Open WebUI as a tool server: uvx mcpo --port 8000 --api-key \"pick-a-secret\" -- /Applications/Atelier.app/Contents/MacOS/atelier-mcp Then add http://localhost:8000 in Open WebUI as an OpenAPI tool server, with the same key. Leave the Terminal window open while you chat."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"claude-desktop","title":"Claude Desktop","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"Choose Claude › Settings…, then Developer in the sidebar, and click Edit Config. It opens ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Add Atelier (if the file already has \"mcpServers\", add only the \"atelier\" part inside it): { \"mcpServers\": { \"atelier\": { \"command\": \"/Applications/Atelier.app/Contents/MacOS/atelier-mcp\", \"args\": [] } } } Quit Claude completely (⌘Q) and open it again. If Atelier doesn't show up, the log is ~/Library/Logs/Claude/mcp-server-atelier.log."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"claude-code","title":"Claude Code","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"claude mcp add --transport stdio --scope user atelier -- /Applications/Atelier.app/Contents/MacOS/atelier-mcp claude mcp get atelier Then type /mcp in Claude Code to see that Atelier is connected."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"codex","title":"Codex","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"Add this to ~/.codex/config.toml (create the file if it isn't there), then start Codex again: [mcp_servers.atelier] command = \"/Applications/Atelier.app/Contents/MacOS/atelier-mcp\" args = [] To turn it off without deleting it, add enabled = false. The Codex desktop app reads the same file."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"phone","title":"From your iPhone","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"Docent on your iPhone can work with the Atelier open on your Mac: search and rate photos, look at them, edit and undo, with the same tools and the same approval cards as Docent on the Mac. The phone connects straight to Atelier over Tailscale, your private network between your own devices, so nothing is opened to Wi-Fi or the internet. Install Tailscale on the iPhone and sign in with the same account as the Mac. The Mac must be awake with Atelier open. Allow a paired phone to use Atelier ✓Ready for phones at roshs-mac.tail1234.ts.net, port 47801. Port47801 Pair a phonePair a Phone… Docent on iPhoneRoPaired Nov 18, 2026 · Last used 2 minutes agoEditAsks you itselfRevoke Turn it on in Atelier › Settings… › Agents › Phone. It's off until you do. If your Mac's firewall asks whether Atelier may accept incoming connections, click Allow. Where the phone finds Atelier: your Mac's Tailscale name. Atelier listens only on the Tailscale address (never on Wi-Fi or Ethernet). Without Tailscale running it says Turn on Tailscale on this Mac to pair a phone, and starts again by itself when Tailscale connects. Pair a Phone… shows a QR code on the Mac's screen for 5 minutes. In Docent on the iPhone, choose Settings › Servers › Add › Scan Pairing Code and point the camera at it. The code works once; when it expires, click New Code. Each paired phone, with when it paired and was last used: What it may do: Look, Organize, Edit or Everything. A new phone gets what Docent on the Mac may do (Edit if "},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"skills","title":"Skills","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"A skill teaches chat apps a workflow in Atelier, such as \"Cull a new import\": a few steps in plain words, with Atelier's commands named in them. Write it once in Atelier › Settings… › Agents › Skills; after that, ask any chat app for the workflow in your own words (\"cull yesterday's import\") and the model reads the skill first, or pick it in Docent by typing /. A skill doesn't change what an app may do: an app allowed Look still can't rate photos, and the skill tells the model so, so it can tell you. Cull a new import 5 actions · 2 filesGroups bursts, picks the sharpest of each and rates the keepers.1 reference needs attention⚠ 1 Share to the shop 1 actionExports the picks at 2048 px for the shop. +−⋯ Each skill with its switch, title, how many commands and files it has, and its description. A skill that's off isn't sent to any app. An orange triangle means a reference needs attention: a command Atelier renamed or removed, or a link to a file that isn't there. Double-click a skill, or select it and press Return, to open it in Edit Skill. + starts a new skill, − moves the selected one to the Trash, and ⋯ has Duplicate, Import Skill… (a skill's folder or its SKILL.md; it arrives switched off and opens in Edit Skill), Export Skill… and Show in Finder."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"edit-skill","title":"Edit Skill","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"Edit Skill opens in its own window. At the top are the Title, the Name apps use (click it to change it), the Description (the model reads it to decide when the skill fits, so say what it does and when), and Arguments: things the app asks you for when you pick the skill, written {{name}} in the steps. On the left are the skill's files: SKILL.md holds the steps, and + adds notes the steps can link to, such as sharpness.md, which the model reads only when it needs them. Double-click a file to rename it; links to it follow. The toolbarAbove the steps: Bold (⌘B), Italic (⌘I), Code (⌘E), Heading, Bulleted List, Numbered List, Quote, Link (⌘K), Insert Command (the same list as /) and Insert Argument ({{name}}, with name selected to type over). Write and Preview are at its right; Preview shows the steps formatted. Commands and toolsEach one shows as a box with an outline, so it can't be mistaken for words you typed: ⌘ marks a command from Atelier's menus, a wrench marks a tool, and an orange ⚠ with the name struck through marks one that needs attention. It counts as one character when you move, select or delete. /Inserts a command or tool: type a few letters of its name (\"rat\" for Set Rating) and press Return or Tab. The list shows Tools first, then each menu, with where the command is in Atelier and a badge: Undo for a change you can undo, Asks first for one Atelier asks you about. ↑ ↓ move, Esc closes the list and leaves the /. The status lineHow many commands the skill names, and "},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"models","title":"Which model","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"Claude and Codex are the most reliable at choosing the right command among many. Local models (Gemma 4, Qwen 3.6 in Ollama) can call tools but miss more often. Atelier keeps their command lists short, and anything risky is confirmed in Atelier's window, not in the chat. Free hosted models in OpenCode vary. Pick one marked for tool use."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"privacy","title":"Privacy","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"The helper talks to Atelier on your Mac only; Atelier makes no network calls for this. With Ollama, nothing leaves your Mac. With a hosted model (Claude, Codex, OpenCode's free models), what Atelier answers goes to that provider as part of the chat. So photo previews and locations are off by default, with switches in Settings › Agents. Private photos are never shared."},{"file":"chat-apps.html","id":"trouble","title":"If it doesn't work","page":"Connect a chat app","text":"“Atelier isn't running”Open Atelier, then ask again. The app isn't allowedSettings › Agents › Apps lists every app that connected. Change Don't Allow to Look or higher. Check Activity for the error, and copy it if you need help. The model never uses AtelierSmall free or local models often answer without calling tools. Ask directly (“use the Atelier tools to list my collections”), or pick a model that supports tool use. The photo doesn't appear in the chatTurn on Let agents see photo previews in Settings › Agents, then ask “show me the photo Atelier is looking at, here in the chat”. The chat app shows it only if it can show images (Docent can; Cherry Studio shows “Image blocked”). Asking to show photos in Atelier selects them in Atelier's own window instead. Connected chat apps pick up the switch without reconnecting. Wrong pathIf Atelier isn't in /Applications, copy the helper's path from Settings › Agents › Connect an App. See also: Settings · Keyboard shortcuts · AI tools"}]};
